"""DECODED NY: original micro-lessons, applications, vocabulary and checks.
Each authored record is a lesson, not a placeholder or a copied exam item.
"""
import json,random
from pathlib import Path
COURSES=[]
def course(id,title,group,description,units):
 c={'id':id,'title':title,'group':group,'description':description,'state':'NY','units':[]}
 for n,(name,rows) in enumerate(units,1):
  u={'id':f'{id}-u{n}','number':n,'title':name,'lessons':[]}
  for j,row in enumerate(rows.strip().split('\n'),1):
   fields=row.split('|')
   assert len(fields)==8,(id,n,j,len(fields))
   title,read,worked,prompt,correct,wrong,why,vocab=fields
   terms=[{'term':a.split('~')[0],'definition':a.split('~')[1]} for a in vocab.split(';')]
   opts=[correct]+wrong.split('~');assert len(opts)==4
   shift=(n+j)%4;opts=opts[shift:]+opts[:shift]
   lid=f'{id}-u{n}-l{j}'
   u['lessons'].append({'id':lid,'title':title,'reading':read,'worked':worked,'application':{'prompt':prompt,'options':opts,'answer':opts.index(correct),'explanation':why},'vocab':terms,'practice':'Explain your choice using the relationship or evidence in this lesson.','reflection':why})
  c['units'].append(u)
 COURSES.append(c)

course('algebra1','Algebra I','Math','Build from equations and functions to quadratics, exponentials, and data.',[
('Quantities, expressions & models', '''Units and precision|A quantity needs both a number and a unit. Dimensional analysis multiplies by conversion factors equal to one. A model should use a sensible domain: counts are whole numbers, while measurements may be continuous.|A 90-minute trip lasts 90/60 = 1.5 hours. At 40 miles per hour, distance is 60 miles.|A runner travels 12 miles in 2 hours. What is the average speed?|6 miles per hour|24 miles per hour~10 miles per hour~14 miles per hour|Divide distance by time, keeping miles in the numerator and hours in the denominator.|Quantity~A measurable property expressed with a number and unit.;Domain~The permitted inputs of a relation or function.
Equivalent expressions|Equivalent expressions have the same value for every allowed input. Distribute multiplication over every term and combine only like terms. A coefficient multiplies a variable expression; an exponent tells a power.|3(x + 4) − 2x = 3x + 12 − 2x = x + 12.|Which expression equals 2(x − 3) + 4x?|6x − 6|6x − 3~2x + 1~6x + 6|Distribution gives 2x − 6 + 4x. Combine the x-terms, keeping the constant −6.|Coefficient~A numerical factor multiplying a variable term.;Equivalent expressions~Expressions with equal values for every permitted input.
Real numbers and radicals|Rational numbers are ratios of integers. Irrational numbers cannot be written that way. Simplify square roots using perfect-square factors. The principal square root is nonnegative, so √(x²) = absolute value of x.|√72 = √(36 × 2) = 6√2, an exact value.|Which is √50 in simplest radical form?|5√2|25√2~10√5~5|Factor 50 as 25 × 2 and take the square root of the perfect-square factor.|Rational number~A number expressible as a ratio of integers with nonzero denominator.;Radicand~The expression inside a radical symbol.'''),
('Linear equations & inequalities','''Equations as balanced relationships|Solve an equation by applying the same reversible operation to both sides. Simplify first, collect variable terms, and isolate the variable. An equation can have one solution, no solution, or all real numbers as solutions.|3x − 5 = 16 becomes 3x = 21, so x = 7.|Solve 4x + 3 = 19.|x = 4|x = 5.5~x = 16~x = 22|Subtract 3 from both sides, then divide by 4. Substitute 4 to check the original equation.|Solution~A value that makes an equation or inequality true.;Identity~An equation true for every allowed value.
Literal equations|A formula relates several quantities. Isolate the requested variable using inverse operations, treating other symbols as constants. State restrictions when you divide by a variable expression.|From A = bh/2, multiply by 2 and divide by nonzero b: h = 2A/b.|Rearrange d = rt for t, when r ≠ 0.|t = d/r|t = dr~t = r/d~t = d − r|Dividing both sides by r isolates the time variable.|Literal equation~An equation containing multiple variables.;Inverse operations~Operations that reverse one another.
Inequalities and intervals|Inequalities describe ranges of values. Multiplying or dividing by a negative number reverses the inequality sign. A strict inequality excludes the endpoint. A compound inequality combines conditions with and or or.|−2x < 8 becomes x > −4. The sign reverses because we divide by −2.|Solve −3x ≥ 12.|x ≤ −4|x ≥ −4~x ≤ 4~x ≥ 4|Division by a negative number reverses the ordering. Check with x = −5, which gives 15 ≥ 12.|Inequality~A statement comparing quantities using an order relation.;Compound inequality~Two or more inequalities joined by and or or.'''),
('Linear functions & systems','''Slope and intercepts|A linear function changes at a constant rate. In y = mx + b, m is slope and b is the y-intercept. Slope is change in output divided by change in input. Vertical lines have undefined slope and are not y-functions.|Through (1,3) and (3,7), slope is (7 − 3)/(3 − 1) = 2.|A line has slope 3 and passes through (0,−2). Its equation is…|y = 3x − 2|y = −2x + 3~y = 3x + 2~x = 3|The point with x = 0 supplies the y-intercept, while the stated slope is the coefficient of x.|Slope~The ratio of vertical change to horizontal change.;Y-intercept~Where a graph meets the y-axis.
Write and compare models|Translate an initial amount into an intercept and a per-unit change into a slope. Compare two linear plans by finding where their outputs match. Interpret the intersection using the original units.|Plan A costs 10 + 2x; Plan B costs 4 + 3x. They cost the same at x = 6.|A taxi charges $5 plus $2 per mile. What is the cost for 4 miles?|$13|$28~$9~$20|The fixed charge is added once: 5 + 2(4) = 13.|Linear model~A model with a constant rate of change.;Rate of change~Output change per unit of input change.
Systems and feasible regions|A solution to a system satisfies every equation or inequality. Use substitution, elimination, or intersection. A system of inequalities has a feasible region rather than just a point; dashed boundaries exclude equality.|y = x + 1 and x + y = 7 give x = 3 and y = 4.|Which ordered pair solves y = 2x and x + y = 9?|(3,6)|(6,3)~(2,7)~(9,18)|Substitute y = 2x: 3x = 9, giving x = 3 and y = 6. Both equations must hold.|System~A collection of equations or inequalities considered simultaneously.;Feasible region~The set satisfying all constraints in a system.'''),
('Functions, sequences & transformations','''Functions and notation|A function assigns exactly one output to each input in its domain. Function notation names an output, not multiplication. Tables and graphs can show functions; the vertical-line test checks whether a graph assigns more than one y-value to an x-value.|If f(x) = x² − 1, then f(3) = 9 − 1 = 8.|For f(x) = 2x + 5, f(−2) equals…|1|−9~−1~9|Replace every x with −2 and follow the order of operations: −4 + 5 = 1.|Function~A relation assigning one output to each permitted input.;Range~The set of outputs of a relation or function.
Arithmetic and geometric sequences|A sequence is a function on ordered integer inputs. Arithmetic sequences add a common difference; geometric sequences multiply by a common ratio. An explicit rule finds a term directly, while a recursive rule uses earlier terms.|The sequence 4, 7, 10 has a common difference of 3 and rule a(n) = 4 + 3(n − 1).|Which is the next term of 3, 6, 12, 24? |48|27~30~36|Each term is twice the preceding term, so multiply 24 by 2.|Arithmetic sequence~A sequence with a constant difference.;Geometric sequence~A sequence with a constant ratio.
Read and transform graphs|Interpret intercepts, increasing intervals, maxima, minima, and end behavior in context. Adding k outside f shifts a graph vertically by k. Replacing x by x − h shifts it right by h. A graph's useful domain may be restricted.|g(x) = f(x) + 3 raises each output by 3 without changing the input.|How does g(x) = f(x) − 4 change the graph?|Down 4 units|Up 4 units~Right 4 units~Left 4 units|Subtracting outside the function changes every output by −4.|Transformation~A rule changing the position or shape of a graph.;Maximum~A greatest function value on a specified domain.'''),
('Polynomials & factoring','''Polynomial structure|A polynomial is a sum of terms with nonnegative integer exponents on its variables. Degree is the greatest term degree after simplification. Add like terms and multiply using distribution. Structure often reveals a useful factorization.|(x + 2)(x + 3) = x² + 5x + 6.|Expand (x + 4)(x − 1).|x² + 3x − 4|x² − 4~x² + 5x − 4~x² + 3x + 4|Multiply all pairs: x² − x + 4x − 4, then combine the x-terms.|Polynomial~A finite sum of terms with nonnegative integer variable exponents.;Degree~The greatest term degree of a nonzero polynomial.
Factor common factors and trinomials|Factoring rewrites a sum as a product. Remove a greatest common factor first. For x² + bx + c, look for numbers whose sum is b and product is c. A difference of squares has form a² − b².|x² − x − 6 = (x − 3)(x + 2).|Factor x² + 5x + 6.|(x + 2)(x + 3)|(x + 1)(x + 6)~(x − 2)(x − 3)~x(x + 5)|The needed pair adds to 5 and multiplies to 6: 2 and 3.|Factor~An expression multiplied with another to form a product.;Greatest common factor~The greatest factor shared by all terms.
Zeros and products|A zero of a function is an input with output zero. The zero-product property applies when a product equals zero. Set each variable factor equal to zero. Dividing by a variable can accidentally remove a valid zero solution.|x(x − 5) = 0 has zeros 0 and 5.|The zeros of (x − 2)(x + 4) are…|2 and −4|−2 and 4~2 and 4~−2 and −4|A product is zero when either factor is zero. Solve x − 2 = 0 and x + 4 = 0.|Zero~An input at which a function equals zero.;Zero-product property~If a product is zero, at least one factor is zero.'''),
('Quadratic equations & functions','''Parabolas and vertex form|A quadratic function has degree two and graphs as a parabola. In y = a(x − h)² + k, the vertex is (h,k) and the symmetry axis is x = h. The sign of a determines whether the vertex is a minimum or maximum.|y = (x − 2)² − 3 has vertex (2,−3) and a minimum of −3.|What is the vertex of y = (x + 1)² + 4?|(-1,4)|(1,4)~(−1,−4)~(4,−1)|Rewrite x + 1 as x − (−1). Vertex form identifies h = −1 and k = 4.|Vertex~The turning point of a parabola.;Axis of symmetry~A line across which a figure reflects onto itself.
Solve quadratics in different ways|Factoring, square roots, completing the square, and the quadratic formula solve quadratic equations. First write standard form when using coefficients. Real solutions correspond to x-intercepts. A negative discriminant means no real roots.|x² − 6x + 5 = 0 factors into (x − 1)(x − 5) = 0.|Solve x² = 49 over the real numbers.|x = −7 or 7|x = 7 only~x = −7 only~x = 24.5|Both 7 and −7 square to 49. The equation has two solutions even though √49 is 7.|Quadratic formula~x = (−b ± √(b² − 4ac))/(2a), for a ≠ 0.;Discriminant~The expression b² − 4ac classifying quadratic roots.
Quadratic models and intersections|Use quadratics for relationships involving squared quantities, area, or constant acceleration. An intersection with a line satisfies both rules. Reject solutions outside a model's meaningful domain, but explain why rather than removing negatives automatically.|For y = x² and y = x + 2, x² − x − 2 = 0 gives intersections (−1,1) and (2,4).|A rectangle has sides x and x + 2 and area 24. Its positive width is…|4|−6~6~12|x(x + 2) = 24 factors into (x + 6)(x − 4) = 0. Width must be positive, so choose 4.|Intersection~A point belonging to each of two graphs.;Constraint~A restriction on allowed values.'''),
('Exponential models','''Growth and decay factors|Exponential models multiply by the same factor over equal input intervals. In y = a(b)^t, a is the initial amount and b the growth factor. A rate r gives factor 1 + r for growth or 1 − r for decay.|A value growing 5% yearly has factor 1.05, not 5.|A $200 item loses 10% yearly. Its value after one year is…|$180|$190~$20~$220|Ten percent of 200 is 20. Subtract it, or multiply 200 by 0.90.|Exponential function~A function with the variable in an exponent and constant positive base.;Decay factor~The fraction retained after each equal time interval.
Linear versus exponential change|A linear pattern adds a fixed amount, whereas an exponential pattern multiplies by a fixed factor. Compare differences and ratios in tables. A percentage change depends on the current amount, so equal percentages need not mean equal numerical changes.|100, 110, 120 is linear; 100, 110, 121 is exponential growth by 10%.|Which table follows exponential growth?|2, 6, 18, 54|2, 5, 8, 11~2, 4, 6, 8~2, 3, 5, 8|Each term is multiplied by 3. Constant multiplication distinguishes this from constant addition.|Common ratio~The multiplier between consecutive terms of a geometric sequence.;Initial value~The output at input zero in a model.
Interpret and compare models|Interpret parameters, long-term trends, and realistic domains before calculating. A sequence of repeated percent changes is multiplicative. Percentage increases and decreases of the same size do not generally cancel because their starting amounts differ.|Increasing 100 by 20% gives 120. Decreasing 120 by 20% gives 96.|After a 50% increase followed by a 50% decrease, $80 becomes…|$60|$80~$40~$120|80 × 1.5 × 0.5 = 60. The decrease applies to the increased amount.|Percent change~A change divided by the original amount, expressed as a percentage.;Parameter~A fixed value controlling a model's behavior.'''),
('Statistics & evidence','''Summarize one-variable data|Use center and spread together. The median resists extreme values better than the mean. Standard deviation describes spread around the mean. Compare distributions using shape, center, spread, and unusual observations; a single statistic is not the whole story.|For 2, 3, 4, 5, 100, the median is 4 while the mean is 22.8.|Which measure of center is usually less affected by one extreme high outlier?|Median|Mean~Range~Maximum|An outlier can strongly move the sum and mean, but the median depends on ordered position.|Median~The middle value of ordered data.;Standard deviation~A measure of typical spread around the mean.
Association and residuals|A scatter plot shows relationships between two quantitative variables. A residual is observed minus predicted output. Correlation describes linear association, not causation. Use context and residual patterns to judge whether a fitted model is reasonable.|If a model predicts 12 but the observation is 15, the residual is 3.|Observed y is 9 and predicted y is 11. The residual is…|−2|2~20~99|Residual = observed − predicted = 9 − 11 = −2.|Residual~Observed output minus predicted output.;Correlation~A measure of the direction and strength of linear association.
Two-way tables and conclusions|Two-way tables organize categorical data. Conditional relative frequencies use the relevant row or column total. A sample can be biased if selection favors a group. An observational association alone cannot establish that changing one variable causes another.|If 12 of 20 bus riders arrive early, 60% of bus riders arrive early.|Of 30 students taking art, 18 also take music. What fraction of art students take music?|18/30|18/100~30/18~12/30|The condition is taking art, so the denominator is the 30 art students.|Conditional frequency~A frequency calculated within a specified subgroup.;Sampling bias~A systematic distortion caused by how a sample is selected.''')])
course('geometry','Geometry','Math','Reason with transformations, proof, similarity, circles, coordinates, and solids.',[
('Foundations & constructions','''Points, lines and angle relationships|Definitions give a proof its starting facts. Segment addition combines adjacent lengths. A linear pair totals 180°, vertical angles are congruent, and triangle interior angles total 180°. A diagram's appearance is not a given.|A triangle with angles 40° and 65° has third angle 75°.|Two supplementary angles include one of 118°. The other is…|62°|28°~118°~242°|Supplementary angles sum to 180°, so subtract 118 from 180.|Supplementary angles~Two angles whose measures sum to 180°.;Congruent~Having the same shape and size.
Midpoints and bisectors|A midpoint divides a segment into congruent halves. A segment bisector must pass through it but need not be perpendicular. An angle bisector creates congruent angles. A perpendicular bisector makes equal halves and a right angle.|The midpoint of (−2,4) and (6,0) is (2,2).|A segment bisector makes lengths 3x + 1 and x + 9. Find x.|4|5~8~10|The halves are equal: 3x + 1 = x + 9, so 2x = 8.|Midpoint~A point dividing a segment into two congruent segments.;Angle bisector~A ray dividing an angle into congruent angles.
Compass and straightedge reasoning|A compass copies distances without numerical measurement. Equal arcs from two endpoints locate points equidistant from them. Joining two such intersections constructs a perpendicular bisector. For angle copying, transfer an arc radius and the chord between its intersections.|To bisect AB, use the same radius greater than half AB from A and B, then join the two arc intersections.|Why must the compass radius exceed half AB in this construction?|To obtain two distinct arc intersections|To measure the angle numerically~To make AB longer~To make the circles tangent|At exactly half the segment length, the circles meet only once; with a smaller radius they do not meet.|Construction~An exact geometric procedure using specified tools.;Perpendicular bisector~A line through a segment's midpoint at 90°.'''),
('Rigid motions & symmetry','''Translations and reflections|Rigid motions preserve lengths and angle measures. A translation moves every point by one vector. A reflection places a point the same perpendicular distance across a line. Orientation reverses under a single reflection but not a translation.|Reflecting (3,−2) across the y-axis gives (−3,−2).|Translate (−1,4) by vector (3,−2).|(2,2)|(−4,6)~(3,−2)~(−2,2)|Add the vector components to the original coordinates.|Rigid motion~A transformation preserving distance and angle measure.;Reflection~A flip across a line with equal perpendicular distances.
Rotations and compositions|A rotation turns points through an angle around a fixed center. About the origin, 90° counterclockwise maps (x,y) to (−y,x). In a composition, order matters; apply the specified first transformation before the next.|Rotating (2,5) by 90° counterclockwise produces (−5,2).|A 180° rotation about the origin maps (−3,1) to…|(3,−1)|(−1,−3)~(1,3)~(−3,−1)|A half-turn reverses both coordinate signs.|Rotation~A turn by a fixed angle about a fixed center.;Composition~Successive application of transformations.
Symmetry and congruence|A figure has symmetry when a transformation maps it onto itself. Congruent figures can be mapped using rigid motions. Match corresponding vertices in order before comparing sides and angles; naming order communicates the correspondence.|A non-square rectangle has 180° rotational symmetry but not 90° rotational symmetry.|Which transformation always preserves congruence?|Translation|Dilation by factor 2~Horizontal stretch~Vertical compression|A translation preserves every distance and angle, unlike a non-unit dilation or stretch.|Line symmetry~Reflection symmetry across a line.;Correspondence~The matching of parts between figures.'''),
('Triangle congruence & proof','''Prove triangles congruent|SSS, SAS, ASA, AAS, and the right-triangle hypotenuse-leg condition can establish congruence. SAS requires the included angle. AAA establishes similarity, not equal size. Unspecified SSA does not establish a unique triangle.|Two side pairs and their included angles establish SAS congruence.|Which is sufficient for triangle congruence?|SAS|AAA~SSA in every case~One equal angle|The included angle fixes the relative placement of the two specified sides.|Congruence criterion~A sufficient set of facts establishing triangle congruence.;Included angle~The angle between two named sides.
Build a logical proof|A proof links givens to a conclusion through justified steps. Shared sides are congruent to themselves by the reflexive property. After proving triangles congruent, corresponding parts can establish a requested equality.|A diagonal shared by two triangles supplies a congruent side pair without an extra given.|After establishing triangle congruence, why may you equate corresponding angles?|Corresponding parts of congruent triangles are congruent|All angles in any triangles are equal~The diagram looks symmetric~AAA proves congruence|Triangle congruence guarantees equality of matched parts, but the correspondence must be correct.|Reflexive property~A quantity or figure is equal or congruent to itself.;Proof~A logically justified argument establishing a conclusion.
Triangle theorems and centers|An isosceles triangle has congruent base angles. A midsegment is parallel to the third side and half its length. Angle bisectors locate the incenter; side perpendicular bisectors locate the circumcenter. Their equal-distance properties refer to different objects.|If a triangle side is 18, its parallel midsegment measures 9.|Which center is equidistant from a triangle's vertices?|Circumcenter|Incenter~Any midpoint~Any point on an angle bisector|The circumcenter lies on the side perpendicular bisectors and is the circumcircle center.|Midsegment~A segment joining midpoints of two triangle sides.;Circumcenter~The point equidistant from the three triangle vertices.'''),
('Similarity & dilations','''Dilations and scale factors|A dilation from a center multiplies each distance from that center by scale factor k. Positive k preserves angle measures; lengths scale by k, areas by k², and volumes by k³. Congruence is the special case k = 1.|A dilation by 3 changes length 4 to 12 and area 5 to 45.|A figure's side lengths double. Its area is multiplied by…|4|2~8~1|Area has two length dimensions, so its scale factor is 2².|Dilation~A transformation scaling distances from a center.;Scale factor~The multiplier relating corresponding lengths.
Prove and use similarity|AA, proportional SSS, or proportional SAS can establish triangle similarity. Use corresponding sides in a consistent order when writing proportions. A parallel line through two triangle sides creates a smaller triangle similar to the whole.|Sides 3,4,5 and 6,8,10 are proportional with scale factor 2.|Similar triangles have corresponding sides 4 and 10, and 6 and x. Find x.|15|12~2.4~16|The scale factor from the first triangle to the second is 10/4 = 2.5, so x = 6(2.5).|Similar figures~Figures with congruent corresponding angles and proportional sides.;Proportion~An equality of two ratios.
Right-triangle similarity|An altitude to a right triangle's hypotenuse creates two smaller triangles similar to the original. The altitude is the geometric mean of the hypotenuse segments. Match the smaller and larger triangles carefully before using other proportions.|If the altitude divides a hypotenuse into 4 and 9, h² = 4 × 9, so h = 6.|A hypotenuse altitude divides the hypotenuse into lengths 3 and 12. Its length is…|6|9~15~36|The altitude satisfies h² = pq = 36; choose the positive length.|Geometric mean~For positive a and b, the number √(ab).;Altitude~A perpendicular segment from a vertex to the line containing the opposite side.'''),
('Right triangles & trigonometry','''Pythagorean and special triangles|In a right triangle, a² + b² = c² with c the hypotenuse. A 45-45-90 triangle has side ratio 1:1:√2, and a 30-60-90 triangle has ratio 1:√3:2. Use exact radicals when requested.|A square of side 7 has diagonal 7√2.|A right triangle has legs 8 and 15. The hypotenuse is…|17|23~7~√161|The sum of squared legs is 64 + 225 = 289; √289 = 17.|Hypotenuse~The side opposite a right triangle's right angle.;Special right triangle~A right triangle with useful fixed side ratios.
Sine, cosine and tangent|Relative to an acute angle, sine is opposite/hypotenuse, cosine is adjacent/hypotenuse, and tangent is opposite/adjacent. The hypotenuse stays fixed, but opposite and adjacent change when the reference angle changes.|For opposite 3 and hypotenuse 5, sin θ = 3/5.|Relative to θ, opposite is 5 and adjacent is 12. tan θ equals…|5/12|12/5~5/13~12/13|Tangent compares the opposite leg with the adjacent leg.|Sine~The opposite-leg to hypotenuse ratio in a right triangle.;Tangent~The opposite-leg to adjacent-leg ratio.
Solve contextual triangles|Draw and label a right triangle before selecting a ratio. Angles of elevation and depression are measured from a horizontal line. Inverse trigonometric functions recover angles from ratios. Use degree mode for degree-based problems and round only at the end.|A 10-meter ladder makes a 60° ground angle; its vertical reach is 10 sin 60°.|A ramp rises 3 meters over a horizontal run of 4 meters. Its angle is…|arctan(3/4)|arcsin(4/3)~arctan(4/3)~cos(3/4)|The known sides are opposite and adjacent, so use inverse tangent of their ratio.|Angle of elevation~The upward angle measured from a horizontal line.;Inverse trigonometric function~A function finding an angle from a trigonometric ratio.'''),
('Coordinates, quadrilaterals & proof','''Distance, midpoint and partition|Coordinate geometry connects algebra and geometric reasoning. Distance follows the Pythagorean theorem. A midpoint averages coordinates. To divide a segment in a specified ratio, move the corresponding fraction of the vector between its endpoints.|One third of the way from (0,0) to (6,9) is (2,3).|The midpoint of (−4,2) and (2,8) is…|(−1,5)|(−2,10)~(3,3)~(−1,3)|Average x-coordinates and y-coordinates separately.|Distance formula~√((x₂−x₁)²+(y₂−y₁)²).;Partition~Divide a segment into parts with a specified ratio.
Parallel and perpendicular lines|Nonvertical parallel lines have equal slopes. Nonzero finite slopes of perpendicular lines multiply to −1. Horizontal and vertical lines form the exceptional perpendicular pair. Use slope plus a point to write a line equation.|A line perpendicular to slope 2 has slope −1/2.|A line perpendicular to y = 3x + 2 has slope…|−1/3|−3~1/3~3|The negative reciprocal of 3 is −1/3.|Perpendicular lines~Lines meeting at right angles.;Negative reciprocal~For nonzero m, the value −1/m.
Classify quadrilaterals with evidence|Use side slopes, lengths, and diagonals to establish a classification. A parallelogram has both opposite side pairs parallel; a rectangle adds right angles, and a rhombus adds four congruent sides. A square has both sets of added properties.|Diagonals that bisect each other establish a parallelogram, not automatically a rectangle.|Which extra fact makes a parallelogram a rectangle?|One right angle|One pair of parallel sides~Equal adjacent sides only~Perpendicular diagonals only|A parallelogram with one right angle has four right angles. Equal sides or perpendicular diagonals alone are not enough.|Parallelogram~A quadrilateral with both opposite side pairs parallel.;Rectangle~A quadrilateral with four right angles.'''),
('Circles & equations','''Arcs, chords and angles|A central angle equals the measure of its intercepted arc. An inscribed angle intercepting the same arc measures half as much. Equal chords subtend equal minor arcs. Tangents meet radii at right angles at the point of tangency.|An inscribed angle intercepting an 80° arc measures 40°.|An inscribed angle intercepts a semicircle. Its measure is…|90°|180°~45°~360°|A semicircle is a 180° arc; an inscribed angle is half its intercepted arc.|Inscribed angle~An angle with vertex on a circle and sides along chords.;Tangent~A line meeting a circle at exactly one point.
Circle equations and lengths|A circle with center (h,k) and radius r satisfies (x − h)² + (y − k)² = r². Convert expanded forms by completing the square. Arc length is rθ when θ is in radians; a sector is the corresponding fraction of the disk.|A circle centered at (2,−1) with radius 3 has equation (x − 2)² + (y + 1)² = 9.|What is the radius of x² + y² = 25?|5|25~12.5~625|The right side is r², so r = √25 = 5.|Radian~An angle unit defined by arc length divided by radius.;Sector~A region bounded by two radii and their included arc.
Intersecting chords and secants|Products of segment lengths relate intersecting chords and secants. For chords inside a circle, one chord's segment product equals the other's. For two secants from an external point, external length times whole length is equal for both.|Chord segments 3 and 8 have product 24; another chord with one segment 4 has the other segment 6.|Intersecting chords have segments 2 and 12 on one chord, and 3 and x on the other. Find x.|8|6~10~24|Use 2 × 12 = 3x, so x = 8.|Chord~A segment with both endpoints on a circle.;Secant~A line intersecting a circle in two points.'''),
('Measurement & solid modeling','''Area and volume relationships|Area uses square units and volume uses cubic units. Prisms and cylinders have volume Bh; pyramids and cones have Bh/3. A sphere has volume 4πr³/3. Identify whether a given measure is a radius or diameter before substituting.|A cylinder of radius 2 and height 5 has volume π(2²)(5) = 20π.|A cone and cylinder have the same base and height. The cone's volume is…|One third of the cylinder's|The same~Three times as large~Half as large|The cone formula includes a factor of 1/3 relative to Bh.|Volume~The amount of three-dimensional space occupied.;Cross-section~A plane's intersection with a solid.
Cross-sections, rotations and density|A solid's cross-section depends on the cutting plane. Rotating a rectangle around one side creates a cylinder. Density is mass divided by volume; population density is people per area. Keep the appropriate units for the context.|A 12-gram object occupying 4 cubic centimeters has density 3 g/cm³.|A rectangle rotates around one of its sides. Which solid is generated?|Cylinder|Sphere~Cone~Pyramid|The opposite side sweeps a circular surface while the rectangle's width becomes the radius.|Density~Mass divided by volume, or count divided by an appropriate measure.;Solid of revolution~A solid generated by rotating a plane region about an axis.
Model and justify a solution|A geometric model makes assumptions about shape, scale, and measurement. Decompose composite solids without double-counting overlap. Explain why a chosen formula fits the shape, and check whether the numerical result and units are reasonable.|A hollow cylinder's material volume is πh(R² − r²), subtracting the inner space from the outer cylinder.|If every length of a solid is multiplied by 3, volume is multiplied by…|27|3~9~6|Volume scales with the cube of the length factor: 3³ = 27.|Geometric model~A mathematical representation using geometric objects and relationships.;Composite solid~A solid made from or decomposed into simpler solids.''')])

course('algebra2','Algebra II','Math','Extend algebra through complex numbers, logarithms, trigonometry, probability, and inference.',[
('Functions & their structure','''Function features and transformations|Analyze domain, range, intercepts, symmetry, and end behavior before calculating. A vertical shift changes outputs; an input shift changes input locations. Even functions satisfy f(−x) = f(x); odd functions satisfy f(−x) = −f(x).|f(x) = x³ is odd, while x² is even.|Which function is even?|f(x) = x²|f(x) = x³~f(x) = x + 1~f(x) = 2x|Replacing x with −x leaves x² unchanged.|Even function~A function satisfying f(−x) = f(x).;End behavior~How outputs behave as inputs grow very positive or negative.
Composition and inverses|Function composition uses one function's output as another's input. An inverse reverses a one-to-one function; its graph reflects across y = x. Restrict a domain when necessary so the inverse is also a function.|For f(x) = 2x + 3, f⁻¹(x) = (x − 3)/2.|If f(x) = x + 2 and g(x) = 3x, then f(g(2)) is…|8|12~10~7|First g(2) = 6; then f(6) = 8. Composition order matters.|Composition~Applying one function to the output of another.;Inverse function~A function reversing a one-to-one function's input-output pairs.
Arithmetic and geometric recursions|An explicit sequence rule uses the term index directly; a recursive rule states initial terms and how to obtain later terms. Arithmetic sequences add a difference; geometric sequences multiply by a ratio. Geometric sums model repeated contributions.|For a₁ = 5 and aₙ = 2aₙ₋₁, the first terms are 5,10,20.|A sequence starts at 7 and adds 4 each time. Its fourth term is…|19|28~23~11|The fourth term is 7 + 3(4), since three changes follow the first term.|Recursive rule~A rule defining terms using previous terms.;Geometric sum~The sum of terms with a common multiplicative ratio.'''),
('Polynomials & complex numbers','''Polynomial operations and division|Add and subtract like terms and multiply through distribution. Polynomial division rewrites a dividend as divisor times quotient plus remainder. A remainder after division by x − a equals f(a), connecting algebraic operations with function evaluation.|Dividing x² − 1 by x − 1 gives quotient x + 1 and remainder 0.|What is the remainder when f(x) = x² + 1 is divided by x − 2?|5|0~3~2|The remainder theorem gives f(2) = 4 + 1 = 5.|Remainder theorem~Division by x − a leaves remainder f(a).;Polynomial quotient~The polynomial result of division before the remainder term.
Factor theorem and multiplicity|If f(a) = 0, then x − a is a factor. Use grouping and identities to factor higher-degree expressions. A real zero of even multiplicity typically touches the x-axis; odd multiplicity crosses it. Multiplicity counts repeated factors.|f(x) = (x − 1)²(x + 2) has zero 1 with multiplicity 2.|If p(4) = 0, which must be a factor of p(x)?|x − 4|x + 4~4x~x² + 4|A zero at 4 corresponds to the factor x − 4.|Factor theorem~f(a) = 0 if and only if x − a is a factor.;Multiplicity~The number of times a factor repeats.
Complex arithmetic and roots|The imaginary unit i satisfies i² = −1. Write complex numbers in a + bi form. Combine real and imaginary parts separately and multiply by distribution. Real-coefficient polynomials have nonreal roots in conjugate pairs.|(2 + 3i) + (1 − i) = 3 + 2i.|What is (3i)²?|−9|9~6i~−3|Square both factors: 9i² = −9.|Imaginary unit~The number i satisfying i² = −1.;Complex conjugates~Numbers a + bi and a − bi.'''),
('Rational & radical relationships','''Rational expressions and restrictions|A rational expression is a quotient of polynomials. Denominators cannot be zero. Factor before canceling common factors, and preserve original restrictions even when a simplified expression hides a removed factor.|(x² − 1)/(x − 1) simplifies to x + 1, but x ≠ 1 remains a restriction.|Simplify (x² − 9)/(x − 3), keeping restrictions.|x + 3, x ≠ 3|x + 3 for all x~x − 3, x ≠ −3~x² + 3|Factor the numerator as (x − 3)(x + 3); the original denominator still excludes 3.|Rational expression~A quotient of two polynomials.;Excluded value~An input outside an expression's original domain.
Rational equations and asymptotes|Clear denominators using a common multiple, but reject forbidden solutions. Vertical asymptotes may occur at uncanceled denominator zeros; canceled factors can create holes. Compare numerator and denominator degrees for polynomial asymptotic behavior.|For 1/x = 1/3, multiplying by nonzero 3x gives x = 3.|A possible solution makes an original denominator zero. What should you do?|Reject it|Accept it after cancellation~Replace the denominator with 1~Round it|A solution must satisfy the original equation where every expression is defined.|Asymptote~A line or curve a graph approaches in specified limits.;Hole~A removable missing point in a graph's domain.
Rational exponents and radical equations|For appropriate real domains, x^(m/n) means an nth root followed by a power. Even roots require nonnegative radicands. Squaring both sides may introduce extraneous solutions, so substitute candidates into the original equation.|√(x + 1) = 3 gives x + 1 = 9, so x = 8.|Solve √(x + 5) = 4.|11|9~−1~21|Square both sides: x + 5 = 16. Check that √16 = 4.|Rational exponent~An exponent expressed as a fraction, relating powers and roots.;Extraneous solution~A candidate produced by a method that fails the original equation.'''),
('Exponential & logarithmic functions','''Exponential models and e|Repeated multiplicative change produces exponential models. In A = P(1 + r/n)^(nt), compounding occurs n times per year. Continuous compounding uses A = Pe^(rt). Interpret rates as decimals and use compatible time units.|A = 100(1.1)^t represents 10% growth per time interval.|What factor represents 8% growth per year?|1.08|0.08~0.92~8|Add the rate 0.08 to 1 to retain the original amount and add its increase.|Exponential model~A relationship involving a variable exponent.;Continuous compounding~Growth modeled by A = Pe^(rt).
Logarithms as inverses|A logarithm gives an exponent: log_b(a) = c means b^c = a, with b positive, b ≠ 1, and a positive. Use product, quotient, and power laws only on their valid domains. Logarithms turn multiplicative relationships into additive ones.|log₂(8) = 3 because 2³ = 8.|log₃(81) equals…|4|27~3~9|The required exponent is 4 because 3⁴ = 81.|Logarithm~The exponent needed to produce an argument from a specified base.;Natural logarithm~A logarithm with base e.
Solve growth and decay problems|Use logarithms to isolate an exponent when a common-base rewrite is unavailable. In half-life models, remaining amount equals initial amount times (1/2)^(t/h). Carry unrounded values through the calculation and interpret the answer's units.|A 40-gram sample after two half-lives contains 40(1/2)² = 10 grams.|A sample starts at 80 grams. After three half-lives, it contains…|10 grams|20 grams~40 grams~30 grams|Halve successively: 80 → 40 → 20 → 10.|Half-life~The time needed for half a radioactive sample to decay.;Change-of-base formula~log_b(a) = ln(a)/ln(b).'''),
('Trigonometric functions','''Radians and the unit circle|A radian measures central angle as arc length divided by radius. On the unit circle, coordinates are (cos θ, sin θ). One full turn is 2π radians. Signs depend on the quadrant, not just a reference angle.|π/2 radians is 90°, with unit-circle coordinates (0,1).|What is cos π?|−1|0~1~π|At π radians, the unit-circle point is (−1,0). Cosine is its x-coordinate.|Unit circle~A circle of radius 1 centered at the origin.;Radian~The angle subtending an arc equal in length to the radius.
Graphs, amplitude and period|For y = A sin(Bx) + D, amplitude is absolute value of A, midline is y = D, and period is 2π/absolute value of B. A horizontal shift changes phase. Model parameters must match the graph's units.|y = 3 sin(2x) + 1 has amplitude 3, period π, and midline y = 1.|The amplitude of y = −4 cos x + 2 is…|4|−4~2~6|Amplitude is a nonnegative distance from midline to peak: absolute value of −4.|Amplitude~Half the vertical distance between a sinusoid's maximum and minimum.;Period~The input interval over which a pattern repeats.
Identities and equations|The identity sin²θ + cos²θ = 1 holds for all θ. Tangent equals sin θ/cos θ where cosine is nonzero. Solving trigonometric equations may require multiple angles within a specified interval; give all solutions in that interval.|On 0 ≤ θ < 2π, sin θ = 0 gives θ = 0 or π.|On 0 ≤ θ < 2π, cos θ = 1 occurs at…|0 only|π only~π/2 only~0 and π|The point with x-coordinate 1 is at angle 0; 2π is excluded by the interval.|Identity~An equation true for every input in its domain.;Reference angle~An acute angle relating an angle to the x-axis.'''),
('Nonlinear systems & modeling','''Systems beyond lines|Intersections satisfy every equation. Substitution can turn a line-parabola system into a quadratic. A system may have zero, one, or multiple real solutions. A graph can suggest solutions, but algebra or substitution verifies them.|y = x² and y = 4 meet at (−2,4) and (2,4).|How many real intersections do y = x² and y = −1 have?|0|1~2~4|A real square cannot be negative, so no x satisfies x² = −1.|Nonlinear system~A system containing at least one nonlinear equation.;Intersection~A common solution to graphical relationships.
Solve and justify inequalities|A polynomial or rational inequality can be analyzed on intervals separated by zeros and undefined points. Test signs in each interval. Endpoint inclusion depends on equality and domain restrictions; a denominator zero is never allowed.|For (x − 1)(x + 2) > 0, the product is positive outside the two roots.|For x² − 9 < 0, which interval is correct?|−3 < x < 3|x > 3 only~x < −3 only~All real x|Between −3 and 3 the square is less than 9; at the endpoints the strict inequality fails.|Sign chart~A record of an expression's sign across intervals.;Critical value~A zero or undefined input separating sign intervals.
Choose and evaluate a model|A model should fit the data pattern and context, not merely pass through points. Compare linear, polynomial, exponential, and periodic structures. Check residuals and prediction limits. Extrapolating far outside observed data increases uncertainty.|Seasonal daylight is better modeled periodically than by an ever-increasing line.|Which model best represents a quantity doubling each equal time interval?|Exponential|Linear~Constant~Uniform random|A constant multiplier of 2 defines exponential growth.|Regression~Fitting a mathematical relationship to data.;Extrapolation~Prediction outside the observed input range.'''),
('Probability','''Sample spaces and counting|A sample space lists possible outcomes. For equally likely outcomes, probability is favorable outcomes divided by total outcomes. Multiply choices at successive stages when each stage's options are known. Outcomes need not be equally likely merely because they can be named.|Two coin flips have outcomes HH, HT, TH, TT when fair flips are independent.|A fair six-sided die is rolled. Probability of an even result is…|1/2|1/6~2/3~1/3|Three of the six equally likely outcomes are even.|Sample space~The set of possible outcomes.;Event~A subset of outcomes.
Conditional probability and independence|Conditional probability restricts the sample space: P(A given B) = P(A and B)/P(B) for P(B) > 0. Independence means knowing B does not change A's probability. Mutually exclusive nonzero-probability events are not independent.|If 8 of 20 seniors play tennis, P(tennis given senior) = 8/20.|For independent events with probabilities 0.4 and 0.5, the probability both occur is…|0.2|0.9~0.1~0.8|Independent probabilities multiply: 0.4 × 0.5 = 0.2.|Conditional probability~Probability within a specified condition.;Independent events~Events whose occurrence does not change each other's probability.
Expected values and simulations|Expected value is a probability-weighted average over many repetitions, not a promised single result. Simulations approximate chance behavior when their random process represents the model appropriately. More trials reduce typical random fluctuation but do not fix bias.|A fair game paying $0 or $10 with equal probability has expected payout $5.|A game pays $12 with probability 1/4 and $0 otherwise. Expected payout is…|$3|$12~$4~$9|Multiply each payout by its probability and add: 12(1/4) + 0(3/4).|Expected value~The probability-weighted mean of outcomes.;Simulation~A process imitating a chance model through repeated trials.'''),
('Statistics & inference','''Normal distributions|A normal distribution is symmetric and bell-shaped. A z-score measures standard deviations from the mean. About 68% of a normal population lies within one standard deviation of the mean. Use a calculator for precise normal areas.|With mean 100 and standard deviation 15, score 130 has z = 2.|For mean 50 and standard deviation 5, a value 40 has z-score…|−2|2~−10~8|z = (40 − 50)/5 = −2.|Z-score~The number of standard deviations a value lies above or below the mean.;Normal distribution~A symmetric bell-shaped probability distribution.
Sampling and experiments|Random sampling supports generalizing to a population. Random assignment supports cause-and-effect conclusions in an experiment. These are different design choices. A large biased sample can still mislead, and association in observational data alone is not causal evidence.|Randomly assigning volunteers to treatments helps separate treatment effects from other differences.|Which design feature supports a causal conclusion about a treatment?|Random assignment|A large convenience sample~A strong correlation alone~A survey of friends|Random assignment balances potential confounders across treatment groups in expectation.|Random sample~A sample selected using chance from a population.;Random assignment~Using chance to allocate treatments.
Estimate uncertainty and interpret evidence|Sample statistics vary from sample to sample. Confidence intervals communicate uncertainty about population parameters. A margin of error is not the probability that an individual response is wrong. Compare observed differences with a chance model before claiming a meaningful effect.|An estimate of 52% with margin 4 percentage points gives interval 48%–56%.|Which interval matches 0.60 ± 0.05?|0.55 to 0.65|0.05 to 0.60~0.60 to 0.95~0.35 to 0.65|Subtract and add the margin to the estimate.|Margin of error~The half-width of an uncertainty interval.;Statistical significance~Evidence that an observed pattern is unlikely under a specified chance model.''')])
course('biology','Life Science: Biology','Science','Investigate cells, energy, inheritance, evolution, ecosystems, and human impacts.',[
('Evidence, chemistry & biological systems','''Investigations and evidence|A scientific explanation connects a claim to observations through reasoning. A controlled investigation changes one proposed cause while measuring a response and accounting for other variables. Repeated measurements and appropriate comparison groups help evaluate uncertainty.|To test light effects on growth, vary light while keeping species, water, soil, and starting conditions comparable.|Which is the dependent variable in a study of light's effect on plant growth?|Measured plant growth|Light level~Plant species held constant~Pot size held constant|The dependent variable is the measured response, while light is the manipulated factor.|Dependent variable~The measured response in an investigation.;Control group~A comparison condition used to interpret an effect.
Water and biological molecules|Water's polarity supports hydrogen bonding, cohesion, and temperature stability. Carbohydrates, lipids, proteins, and nucleic acids perform different but overlapping roles. Dehydration reactions join subunits; hydrolysis uses water to cleave links. A molecule's structure influences its function.|A protein's amino acid sequence influences how it folds; extreme conditions can disrupt that fold.|Which reaction uses water to split a polymer's linkage?|Hydrolysis|Dehydration synthesis~DNA replication~Diffusion|Hydrolysis consumes water during cleavage, adding components of water to the resulting pieces.|Hydrolysis~Cleavage of a linkage through reaction with water.;Polarity~An uneven distribution of electrical charge in a molecule.'''),
('Cells, transport & enzymes','''Cell organization and membranes|Cells are the basic units of living systems. Membranes regulate exchange; DNA stores information; ribosomes build proteins. Eukaryotic cells contain membrane-bound organelles. Diffusion moves particles down concentration gradients; active transport can move them against gradients using energy.|A cell in a hypertonic solution tends to lose water through osmosis.|Water crosses a selectively permeable membrane toward the side with more nonpenetrating solute. This is…|Osmosis|Active transport in all cases~Mitosis~Translation|Osmosis is net water movement across a selectively permeable membrane due to water-potential differences.|Osmosis~Net water movement across a selectively permeable membrane.;Organelle~A specialized structure within a cell.
Enzymes and reaction rates|Enzymes are catalysts that lower activation energy without being consumed. Their active sites interact with substrates. Temperature, pH, and substrate concentration can affect activity; excessive heat or unsuitable pH may alter shape and reduce function.|An enzyme assay compares product formed per minute under controlled temperatures.|What does an enzyme lower?|Activation energy|The total energy of all products~The amount of matter conserved~The number of atoms in the substrate|A catalyst provides a lower-activation-energy pathway; it does not change the conservation of matter.|Enzyme~A biological catalyst.;Substrate~A reactant acted upon by an enzyme.'''),
('Energy & matter in organisms','''Photosynthesis|Photosynthesis uses light energy to build energy-rich organic molecules from carbon dioxide and water, releasing oxygen in oxygenic photosynthesis. Matter is rearranged rather than created. Most plant dry biomass comes from carbon fixed from atmospheric carbon dioxide, not soil minerals alone.|Tracing carbon atoms from CO₂ into sugar shows where new plant biomass originates.|Most carbon in a growing tree's organic molecules originally comes from…|Atmospheric carbon dioxide|Soil rocks alone~Sunlight as matter~Oxygen gas only|Photosynthesis incorporates carbon from carbon dioxide into organic molecules. Light supplies energy, not carbon atoms.|Photosynthesis~Conversion of light energy into chemical energy in organic molecules.;Biomass~The mass of living or recently living biological material.
Cellular respiration|Cellular respiration transfers energy from organic molecules to ATP through linked reactions. Aerobic respiration uses oxygen and produces carbon dioxide and water. Plants respire as well as photosynthesize. Energy moves and disperses as heat; atoms continue cycling.|A plant kept in darkness can continue respiration using stored organic molecules.|Which organisms carry out cellular respiration?|Plants and animals|Animals only~Plants only in sunlight~Neither plants nor animals|Both plants and animals need usable energy from organic molecules. Respiration is distinct from gas exchange alone.|ATP~A molecule used for energy transfer in cells.;Cellular respiration~A process transferring chemical energy from organic molecules into usable cellular forms.'''),
('Body systems & homeostasis','''Interacting body systems|Specialized cells form tissues, organs, and systems. Digestive systems supply small molecules, respiratory surfaces exchange gases, and circulatory systems distribute materials. Function depends on coordinated transport rather than one system acting alone.|Oxygen enters blood at lung surfaces and is transported to cells where it supports aerobic respiration.|Which pair directly links oxygen uptake to delivery throughout the body?|Respiratory and circulatory|Digestive and skeletal only~Nervous and reproductive only~Integumentary and urinary only|Respiratory surfaces exchange gases and the circulatory system transports oxygen to tissues.|Tissue~A group of cells performing related functions.;Organ system~A group of organs coordinating major functions.
Feedback and regulation|Homeostasis maintains internal conditions within workable ranges. Negative feedback opposes a deviation; positive feedback amplifies a process until an endpoint. Sensors, signals, and effectors form regulatory pathways. A stable condition is dynamic, not motionless.|As body temperature rises, sweating can increase heat loss and oppose the rise.|Sweating in response to overheating is an example of…|Negative feedback|Positive feedback~Natural selection within minutes~No regulation|The response counteracts the original temperature increase.|Homeostasis~Regulation of internal conditions within viable ranges.;Negative feedback~A response that opposes a change from a regulated condition.'''),
('Cell division, development & reproduction','''Mitosis and differentiation|Mitosis partitions duplicated chromosomes into two nuclei, supporting growth and repair. Most body cells contain the same genome, but different genes are expressed in different cells. Differentiation produces specialized cell types; uncontrolled division can contribute to cancer.|A muscle cell and nerve cell use different gene-expression patterns despite sharing most DNA sequences.|Why can cells with the same DNA perform different functions?|They express different sets of genes|Every differentiated cell has no DNA~All cells make identical proteins~DNA is converted completely into protein|Gene regulation determines which proteins and regulatory products a cell produces.|Mitosis~Nuclear division producing genetically similar daughter nuclei.;Differentiation~Development of specialized cell structures and functions.
Meiosis and reproductive variation|Meiosis produces haploid cells through two divisions after one DNA replication. Crossing over and independent assortment contribute to variation. Fertilization combines gametes and restores the diploid chromosome number. Mutations in reproductive lineages can be inherited.|A diploid organism with 12 chromosomes normally makes gametes with 6.|Meiosis normally changes a diploid chromosome number from 20 to…|10 in each gamete|20 in each gamete~40 in each gamete~5 in every body cell|Meiosis halves the chromosome-set number. Fertilization restores the diploid number.|Meiosis~A division process reducing chromosome sets for sexual reproduction.;Gamete~A haploid reproductive cell.'''),
('DNA, inheritance & biotechnology','''From DNA to protein|DNA sequence information can be transcribed into RNA and translated into protein. DNA base pairing supports replication. The genetic code relates RNA codons to amino acids; a mutation may change a protein, regulation, or neither. Traits also depend on environment.|A substitution changes a codon, but the altered codon may still specify the same amino acid.|During translation, a ribosome uses information in…|mRNA|A phospholipid bilayer~Starch alone~An ecosystem pyramid|mRNA codons guide amino acid assembly at ribosomes.|Transcription~Production of RNA using a DNA template.;Translation~Assembly of a polypeptide using an RNA sequence.
Inheritance patterns and genetic evidence|Alleles are gene variants. In a simple dominant-recessive model, a heterozygote expresses the dominant phenotype. Real traits may involve multiple alleles, codominance, many genes, and environment. Pedigrees, probability models, and DNA evidence answer different genetic questions.|Aa × Aa predicts genotype proportions 1 AA : 2 Aa : 1 aa under the simple model.|For Aa × Aa, the probability of aa is…|1/4|1/2~3/4~1|Each parent contributes a with probability 1/2; independent contributions multiply to 1/4.|Allele~A variant of a gene.;Genotype~An organism's genetic constitution for specified loci.'''),
('Evolution & diversity','''Natural selection and adaptation|Heritable variation exists within populations. When variants affect reproductive success in an environment, allele frequencies can change across generations. Selection does not produce a trait because an organism needs it; mutations do not target future needs.|Antibiotic exposure can favor bacteria already carrying heritable resistance variants.|Natural selection directly changes populations across generations because individuals differ in…|Heritable reproductive success|Their desire to adapt~Their need for mutations~Their ability to choose new alleles|Inherited variants associated with greater reproductive success can become more common.|Natural selection~Differential reproductive success associated with heritable variation.;Adaptation~A heritable feature shaped by selection in an environment.
Evidence, ancestry and speciation|Fossils, homologous structures, DNA comparisons, and biogeography support common ancestry. Speciation involves divergence and reproductive isolation. A phylogenetic tree represents a hypothesis of relationships; tips next to each other are not necessarily the most closely related without considering branch points.|Two species sharing a more recent common ancestor are more closely related on a phylogeny.|Which evidence most directly compares inherited molecular similarities?|DNA sequence comparisons|Similar diets alone~Identical geographic size~Equal individual ages|DNA similarities can be compared as inherited evidence of ancestry, alongside other evidence.|Common ancestor~An ancestral population shared by descendant lineages.;Speciation~The formation of distinct evolutionary lineages with reproductive isolation.'''),
('Ecosystem dynamics','''Food webs, energy and matter|Energy enters most ecosystems through producers, moves through feeding relationships, and is dissipated as heat. Atoms cycle through organisms and the physical environment. Decomposers return nutrients to usable pools. Energy transfer is limited, so higher trophic levels support less biomass in many systems.|Carbon can move from air to a plant to an animal and back through respiration.|Which statement distinguishes energy from matter in ecosystems?|Energy flows and dissipates while matter cycles|Both are created by consumers~Matter disappears after respiration~Energy cycles without losses|Atoms are reused, while useful energy progressively disperses as heat and requires continued input.|Trophic level~A feeding position in a food web.;Decomposer~An organism breaking down dead material and recycling nutrients.
Population interactions and carrying capacity|Population growth depends on resources, predation, disease, competition, and environmental conditions. Carrying capacity can change over time. Models simplify these interactions; apparent correlation alone may not establish a causal relationship between two populations.|A drought can lower available food and reduce the carrying capacity for herbivores.|Which change would most likely lower an area's carrying capacity for deer?|A sustained loss of vegetation|More available forage~A larger accessible habitat~Removal of a limiting disease|Less vegetation reduces the food resources supporting the population.|Carrying capacity~The population size an environment can sustain under given conditions.;Limiting factor~A condition restricting population growth.'''),
('Human impacts & biological design','''Biodiversity and ecosystem resilience|Genetic, species, and ecosystem diversity support different aspects of resilience. Habitat loss, invasive species, pollution, and overharvesting can disrupt interactions. Conservation decisions should compare evidence, costs, benefits, and effects at multiple scales rather than rely on one indicator.|A wildlife corridor can reconnect habitats, allowing movement and gene flow between populations.|A habitat corridor most directly helps by…|Connecting separated populations|Eliminating every predator~Preventing all mutations~Making every species identical|Connectivity can support movement and exchange of alleles between isolated groups.|Biodiversity~Biological variety across genes, species, and ecosystems.;Resilience~A system's capacity to withstand or recover from disturbance.
Design and evaluate a solution|An engineering solution is judged against criteria and constraints using evidence. Biological problems may involve disease transmission, resource use, or ecosystem restoration. Compare alternatives and unintended consequences. Revise a design after testing rather than treating the first result as final.|Compare filtration designs using removal efficiency, cost, and effects on beneficial organisms.|Which is a constraint in designing a water filter?|A fixed budget|The desired removal efficiency~The goal of cleaner water~The research question alone|A constraint limits possible designs; a performance target is a criterion.|Criterion~A desired measure of success for a design.;Constraint~A limit on possible designs or solutions.''')])

course('earth','Earth and Space Sciences','Science','Connect cosmic history, Earth systems, geology, weather, climate, and sustainable decisions.',[
('Evidence, maps & system models','''Scale, measurement and models|Earth scientists combine observations at different spatial and temporal scales. A model is a purposeful simplification with assumptions and limits. Compare claims with data and account for measurement uncertainty. Patterns can reveal a process without making every correlation causal.|A rock sample, satellite image, and global climate record represent different scales of evidence.|Which is strongest support for a scientific claim?|Multiple relevant, reproducible observations|A single unsupported opinion~A visually attractive diagram alone~Agreement without evidence|Evidence must address the claim and be open to checking or replication.|Model~A simplified representation used to explain or predict.;Scale~The spatial or temporal extent of an observation.
Coordinates, contours and gradients|Latitude measures north-south position and longitude east-west position. Contour lines connect equal elevations. Closely spaced contours indicate steeper slopes; a gradient is change in a field quantity divided by distance. Read map intervals and units before calculating.|A rise of 100 meters over 2 kilometers has gradient 50 meters per kilometer.|Closer contour lines generally indicate…|A steeper slope|A gentler slope~Lower elevation in every case~No elevation change|The same elevation change occurs over a shorter horizontal distance.|Contour line~A line joining locations with equal elevation.;Gradient~Change in a field value divided by distance.'''),
('The universe & stellar evolution','''Cosmic evidence and stars|Light carries information about composition, motion, and temperature. Stellar spectra reveal elements and Doppler shifts. Galaxy redshifts support expansion of the universe. Stars form when gravity gathers material; fusion changes nuclei and releases energy.|Spectral lines shifted toward longer wavelengths indicate recession in a Doppler interpretation.|A star's emission spectrum most directly helps identify…|Its chemical composition|Its exact planet count~Its age without a model~Its surface rocks|Atoms and ions produce characteristic spectral features.|Spectrum~Radiation separated by wavelength or frequency.;Redshift~A shift of spectral features toward longer wavelengths.
Stellar lifecycles and elements|A star's mass strongly affects its lifetime and eventual fate. Nuclear fusion builds heavier nuclei up to limits set by stellar processes; some heavier elements require other energetic events. The Sun's energy originates from hydrogen fusion, not ordinary burning.|The Sun fuses hydrogen into helium while releasing energy.|The Sun's main energy source is…|Nuclear fusion|Chemical combustion~Friction with space~Fission of crustal rocks|Fusion of light nuclei releases energy in the Sun's core.|Fusion~Combining light nuclei into heavier nuclei.;Stellar evolution~Changes in stars over their lifetimes.'''),
('Solar system & orbital patterns','''Gravity and planetary motion|Gravity acts between masses and supports orbital motion. An orbit is continuous motion under gravity rather than a state with no gravity. Planetary orbits are approximately ellipses. Orbital speed varies with position; objects nearer a central body move faster along a given elliptical orbit.|Earth moves faster near perihelion than near aphelion.|At the closest point of an elliptical orbit, an object generally moves…|Fastest|Slowest~With zero gravity~In a straight unchanging path|Gravitational orbital dynamics connect smaller orbital distance with greater speed along that orbit.|Orbit~A path followed under gravitational interaction.;Ellipse~A closed curve with two focal points.
Earth, Moon and Sun cycles|Earth's rotation produces daily apparent sky motion. Its tilted axis and revolution produce seasons, not changing distance from the Sun. Moon phases depend on viewing illuminated portions; eclipses require alignment and do not occur at every new or full Moon.|Northern Hemisphere summer occurs when that hemisphere is tilted toward the Sun.|What primarily causes Earth's seasons?|Axial tilt combined with revolution|Large seasonal distance changes~Changes in the Sun's daily size~Moon phases|Tilt changes sunlight angle and day length through the year.|Axial tilt~The angle between a rotation axis and the perpendicular to an orbital plane.;Lunar phase~The visible fraction and arrangement of the Moon's illuminated face.'''),
('Materials, rocks & geologic time','''Minerals and the rock cycle|Minerals have characteristic compositions and structures. Igneous rocks form from solidified melt, sedimentary rocks from accumulated material or precipitation, and metamorphic rocks through solid-state changes. Weathering, erosion, heat, pressure, melting, and crystallization connect these categories.|A rock recrystallized by heat and pressure without melting is metamorphic.|Which process produces an igneous rock?|Solidification of melt|Compaction of all sediments~Solid-state metamorphism only~Evaporation of every mineral|Cooling and crystallization of magma or lava produce igneous rocks.|Mineral~A naturally occurring inorganic solid with a characteristic structure and composition.;Metamorphism~Solid-state alteration by heat, pressure, or fluids.
Relative and numerical dating|Superposition orders undeformed sedimentary layers from older below to younger above. Cross-cutting structures are younger than the rocks they cut. Radioactive decay supplies numerical ages under appropriate assumptions. Fossils and unconformities help correlate incomplete records.|A fault cutting three rock layers formed after those layers were deposited.|A dike cuts layers A and B. Which is necessarily true?|The dike is younger than both layers|The dike is older than both~Layer A must be youngest~They formed simultaneously|A cross-cutting feature must postdate the material it cuts.|Unconformity~A surface representing missing geologic time.;Half-life~The time required for half of a radioactive isotope population to decay.'''),
('Plate tectonics & Earth hazards','''Plate boundaries and evidence|Lithospheric plates move over the underlying mantle. Interior heat supports mantle convection; seismic-wave patterns help infer layered internal structure. The mantle is mostly solid but can flow over geologic time. Divergent boundaries form new crust; convergent boundaries can subduct or build mountains; transform boundaries accommodate sideways motion. Earthquake patterns, magnetic stripes, and rock ages support plate tectonics.|Ocean-floor rocks generally become older farther from a mid-ocean ridge.|New oceanic crust commonly forms at…|Divergent boundaries|All transform faults~Every continental interior~Only collision mountains|Mantle-derived melt rises and solidifies as plates move apart at ridges.|Subduction~One plate descending beneath another.;Plate tectonics~The theory of moving lithospheric plates and their interactions.
Earthquakes, volcanoes and risk|Earthquakes release energy through seismic waves. Wave arrival patterns help locate events; hazard differs from risk because exposure and vulnerability matter. Volcano behavior depends on magma and setting. Design solutions reduce impacts but cannot eliminate all uncertainty.|Building codes and evacuation plans can reduce losses without preventing tectonic activity.|Which action reduces earthquake risk rather than stopping earthquakes?|Retrofitting vulnerable buildings|Turning off plate motion~Removing all mantle heat~Changing Earth's orbital tilt|Retrofitting reduces vulnerability, one component of risk.|Seismic wave~Energy traveling through or along Earth after a disturbance.;Risk~Potential harm considering hazard, exposure, and vulnerability.'''),
('Surface processes & water','''Weathering, erosion and deposition|Weathering breaks or alters material in place; erosion transports it; deposition leaves it behind. Water, wind, ice, and gravity act differently across particle sizes and landscapes. Transport capacity depends on the moving medium's energy, not just particle availability.|As a stream slows, it can deposit larger particles it no longer carries.|Which process is transport of sediment by a river?|Erosion|Crystallization~Weathering only~Metamorphism|Erosion includes removal and movement; deposition is settling after transport.|Erosion~Removal and transport of Earth material.;Deposition~Accumulation of transported sediment.
The water cycle and reservoirs|Water moves among atmosphere, surface, groundwater, ice, and organisms. Solar energy drives evaporation and gravity drives downward flow. Permeability and porosity influence infiltration and groundwater storage. Human withdrawal can exceed replenishment.|Paving a permeable area often increases runoff and reduces infiltration.|Replacing soil with impermeable pavement usually increases…|Surface runoff|Infiltration~Groundwater recharge in all cases~Soil pore volume|Impermeable surfaces prevent some water from entering the ground.|Infiltration~Entry of water into the ground.;Aquifer~A permeable geologic unit capable of storing and transmitting groundwater.'''),
('Weather & atmospheric circulation','''Energy, pressure and winds|Unequal solar heating drives atmospheric circulation. Warm air can expand and rise, and pressure differences help drive winds. Earth's rotation deflects large-scale motion through the Coriolis effect. Land and water heat differently, producing local circulation patterns.|During a typical sunny day, a sea breeze can move from cooler water toward warmer land.|Winds are primarily driven by…|Air-pressure differences|Identical temperatures everywhere~Moonlight alone~Changes in mineral hardness|Pressure gradients exert forces on air; rotation and surface effects alter the resulting flow.|Air pressure~Force exerted by air per unit area.;Coriolis effect~Apparent deflection associated with observing motion on a rotating Earth.
Moisture, fronts and forecasting|Relative humidity compares actual water vapor with the amount associated with saturation at that temperature. Cooling air can cause condensation. Fronts separate air masses; their movement helps explain changing weather. Forecasts combine observations with models and express uncertainty.|Air cooled to its dew point can begin condensing water vapor into droplets.|Cloud droplets commonly form when air…|Cools to saturation|Loses all water vapor~Has no particles in any circumstances~Becomes uniformly hotter without limit|Cooling reduces the saturation vapor pressure and can make condensation favorable.|Dew point~The temperature at which air becomes saturated when cooled at constant pressure and vapor content.;Front~A boundary between air masses.'''),
('Climate & Earth-system feedbacks','''Climate patterns and evidence|Climate describes distributions of weather over longer periods. Latitude, altitude, oceans, topography, and circulation influence regional patterns. Ice cores, sediments, and other proxies extend records beyond instruments. A short cold event does not disprove a long-term warming trend.|A coastal region may have smaller annual temperature swings than a similar inland region.|Which describes climate rather than a single weather event?|A region's 30-year precipitation pattern|Today's thunderstorm~Tomorrow's wind forecast~This afternoon's temperature|Climate concerns long-term statistical patterns rather than one event.|Climate~Long-term patterns and statistics of weather.;Proxy~An indirect record used to infer past conditions.
Carbon, energy and feedback|Greenhouse gases absorb and emit infrared radiation, affecting Earth's energy balance. Carbon cycles among air, ocean, life, and rocks. Feedback can amplify or oppose a change. Reduced ice reflectivity can amplify warming by increasing absorbed solar energy.|Melting reflective ice can expose darker surfaces that absorb more sunlight.|Ice melt exposing dark ocean is usually a…|Positive warming feedback|Negative warming feedback~Proof of no energy transfer~Source of new solar energy|Darker surfaces absorb more energy, reinforcing the initial warming.|Albedo~The fraction of incoming radiation reflected by a surface.;Feedback~A response that amplifies or opposes an initial change.'''),
('Resources, sustainability & design','''Resources and human impacts|Resources are unevenly distributed by geologic processes. Extraction and use have environmental and social effects. Renewable does not mean impact-free or unlimited. Evaluate evidence for benefits, costs, access, and long-term effects before comparing resource strategies.|Groundwater can be depleted even though the water cycle continually operates.|A renewable resource can be depleted locally when…|Use exceeds replenishment|It receives any sunlight~It has economic value~It is measured carefully|Renewability describes replenishment processes, not a guarantee of unlimited supply at any location.|Renewable resource~A resource replenished on human-relevant timescales under suitable conditions.;Sustainability~Meeting needs while maintaining future environmental and resource capacity.
Evaluate solutions across systems|An Earth-system solution may shift effects among atmosphere, water, land, and communities. Define criteria and constraints, test alternatives, and compare trade-offs with data. Hazard reduction and climate mitigation are different goals and can require complementary designs.|A flood barrier may protect one neighborhood while altering water flow elsewhere.|Which comparison best evaluates a proposed flood design?|Risk reduction, cost, habitat effects, and downstream impacts|Appearance alone~The number of supporters alone~A guarantee of zero future rain|Multiple criteria reveal trade-offs and effects beyond the immediate site.|Mitigation~Action reducing a hazard's impact or the drivers of a problem.;Trade-off~A choice gaining one benefit while giving up another.''')])
course('chemistry','Physical Science: Chemistry','Science','Use particle models and evidence to explain matter, reactions, energy, and chemical systems.',[
('Measurement & particle models','''Quantitative evidence and units|Chemistry connects observable changes to particle-level explanations and mathematical models. Use units, appropriate precision, and controlled comparisons. Density is mass divided by volume; uncertainty limits what a measurement can establish.|A 24-gram sample occupying 8 mL has density 3 g/mL.|A 10-gram sample has volume 5 mL. Its density is…|2 g/mL|50 g/mL~0.5 g/mL~15 g/mL|Divide mass by volume: 10/5 = 2, retaining the compound unit.|Density~Mass per unit volume.;Uncertainty~A quantified or described limit on the precision or reliability of a result.
States, mixtures and separation|Particles in solids, liquids, and gases differ in arrangement and motion. An element contains one atomic number; a compound has chemically combined elements. Mixtures retain component identities and can be separated using physical-property differences.|Filtration separates an insoluble solid from a liquid, but not dissolved salt from water.|Which method can recover dissolved salt from saltwater?|Evaporate the water|Ordinary filtration alone~Use a magnet on all salts~Freeze the salt into iron|Evaporation removes the solvent, leaving the nonvolatile dissolved solid.|Compound~A substance of chemically combined elements in fixed proportions.;Mixture~A physical combination of substances with variable composition.'''),
('Atomic structure & nuclear processes','''Atoms, ions and isotopes|Atomic number is proton count and identifies an element. Isotopes have different neutron counts. Ions differ in electron count from neutral atoms. Average atomic mass is a weighted mean of isotope masses and abundances, not the mass of every atom.|Carbon-14 has 6 protons and 8 neutrons.|An atom with 11 protons and 10 electrons has charge…|+1|−1~0~+21|One more proton than electron gives a net charge of +1.|Isotope~Atoms of one element with different neutron numbers.;Ion~An atom or group with net charge.
Nuclear change and energy|Nuclear reactions change nuclei and may change element identity. Conserve mass number and charge when balancing nuclear equations. Radioactive decay has probabilistic individual events but predictable population half-lives. Nuclear processes differ from chemical rearrangements of electrons and bonds.|After two half-lives, one quarter of an original isotope population remains on average.|A 32-gram isotope sample undergoes three half-lives. How much original isotope remains?|4 grams|8 grams~16 grams~2 grams|Halve the original-isotope amount three times: 32 → 16 → 8 → 4.|Radioactive decay~Spontaneous transformation of an unstable nucleus.;Fission~Splitting a heavy nucleus into smaller nuclei.'''),
('Electrons & periodic patterns','''Electrons and light|Electrons occupy quantized energy states. Absorbing suitable energy can excite an electron; transitions to lower states release photons. Photon energy increases with frequency. Emission and absorption spectra reveal characteristic energy differences rather than continuous orbital tracks.|A higher-frequency photon has more energy because E = hf.|An electron falling to a lower energy state typically…|Emits energy|Absorbs energy necessarily~Changes a proton into a neutron~Becomes the nucleus|The atom releases the energy difference, often as a photon.|Excited state~An electron configuration above the lowest-energy arrangement.;Photon~A quantum of electromagnetic radiation.
Periodic trends and evidence|Elements in a group have related valence-electron patterns. Across a period, nuclear charge and shielding help explain trends in radius and ionization energy. Trends have exceptions; use relevant data rather than treating them as rules without limits.|Removing an electron generally requires more energy when it is more strongly attracted to the nucleus.|Elements in the same main-group column often have similar chemistry because of similar…|Valence-electron patterns|Atomic masses exactly~Neutron counts~Numbers of occupied shells in all cases|Valence electrons strongly influence bonding and reactions.|Ionization energy~Energy needed to remove an electron from a gaseous species.;Valence electron~An outer-shell electron involved in chemical behavior.'''),
('Bonding, structure & properties','''Ionic, covalent and metallic models|Bonding models explain how particles interact. Ionic substances contain oppositely charged ions; covalent bonds share electron density; metallic bonding involves mobile electrons within a metal structure. Formula units and molecules are not interchangeable descriptions of all substances.|Molten ionic compounds can conduct because ions move; an ionic crystal's ions are usually fixed in place.|Why can molten sodium chloride conduct electricity?|Its ions can move|Its atoms turn into protons~All its bonds disappear into nothing~It contains free neutrons|Mobile charged particles transport charge through the melt.|Covalent bond~A bond involving shared electron density.;Ionic bond~Electrostatic attraction between oppositely charged ions.
Polarity and intermolecular forces|Molecular polarity depends on bond polarity and geometry. Intermolecul…1489 tokens truncated…track electron transfer and charge balance. Voltaic cells use spontaneous reactions to produce electrical energy; electrolytic cells use electrical input to drive reactions. Ions and electrons have different paths.|Zn → Zn²⁺ + 2e⁻ is oxidation.|A species gaining electrons is undergoing…|Reduction|Oxidation~Neutralization in every case~Fusion|Reduction is electron gain; oxidation is electron loss.|Oxidation~Loss of electrons.;Reduction~Gain of electrons.'''),
('Carbon chemistry & materials design','''Carbon structures and reactions|Carbon can form chains, rings, and varied functional groups. Structure influences properties, reactivity, and biological function. Isomers share a formula but differ in arrangement. Polymerization links smaller units; a useful material's behavior depends on molecular structure and interactions.|Ethanol and dimethyl ether share a molecular formula but have different structures and properties.|Compounds with the same molecular formula but different arrangements are…|Isomers|Isotopes~Identical compounds necessarily~Only ions|Isomers differ structurally despite having the same atom counts.|Isomer~A compound sharing a formula with another but differing in arrangement.;Functional group~A structural group associated with characteristic chemical behavior.
Evaluate chemical technologies|A material or chemical process must satisfy performance criteria within constraints. Evaluate energy use, toxicity, resources, waste, and lifespan rather than one property alone. Scientific evidence supports comparisons, while value judgments may determine which trade-offs are acceptable.|Compare battery materials by capacity, safety, resource availability, and recyclability.|Which approach best evaluates a new packaging material?|Compare performance and lifecycle impacts|Consider color only~Assume renewable means harmless~Ignore disposal|A useful comparison considers multiple stages and outcomes, including trade-offs.|Lifecycle~The stages from resource extraction through use and disposal or reuse.;Design criterion~A measurable desired performance of a solution.''')])

course('physics','Physical Science: Physics','Science','Explain motion, interactions, energy, waves, electricity, and modern physical evidence.',[
('Measurement, vectors & graphs','''Scalars, vectors and uncertainty|Scalars have magnitude, while vectors include direction. Displacement differs from path length. Represent vector components consistently and attach units to quantities. Measurements and models have uncertainty; numerical precision should match the evidence.|Traveling 3 m east then 3 m west gives 6 m distance but zero displacement.|Which quantity is a vector?|Velocity|Mass~Temperature~Elapsed time|Velocity specifies both speed and direction, unlike the listed scalars.|Vector~A quantity with magnitude and direction.;Displacement~The vector change in position.
Position and velocity graphs|Slope on a position-time graph is velocity. Slope on a velocity-time graph is acceleration, and signed area gives displacement. Interpret axes before reasoning from a shape: a flat line means different things on different graphs.|A position increase of 12 m over 3 s gives average velocity 4 m/s.|A horizontal velocity-time line at +5 m/s means…|Constant positive velocity|Zero speed~Increasing acceleration~Position fixed|The velocity is unchanged at +5; the object keeps changing position.|Velocity~Rate of change of position.;Acceleration~Rate of change of velocity.'''),
('Motion in one and two dimensions','''Constant acceleration and free fall|Constant-acceleration equations connect displacement, time, velocity, and acceleration. Near Earth's surface, ideal free fall has downward acceleration about 9.8 m/s² regardless of mass when air resistance is negligible. Choose a sign convention before substituting.|Starting from rest at 2 m/s², velocity after 3 s is 6 m/s.|An object starts from rest with acceleration 4 m/s² for 2 s. Its final velocity is…|8 m/s|2 m/s~4 m/s~16 m/s|v = v₀ + at = 0 + 4(2).|Free fall~Motion under gravity alone.;Initial velocity~Velocity at the chosen starting time.
Projectile and circular motion|In ideal projectile motion, horizontal and vertical components evolve independently. Uniform circular motion has constant speed but changing velocity because direction changes. Its acceleration points toward the circle's center and requires a net inward force.|A horizontally launched projectile accelerates vertically but not horizontally in the ideal model.|Uniform circular motion has acceleration directed…|Toward the center|Along the outward radius~Always along the tangent~Nowhere because speed is constant|Velocity changes direction, requiring centripetal acceleration inward.|Projectile~An object moving under gravity after launch, neglecting other forces in the ideal model.;Centripetal acceleration~Inward acceleration associated with circular motion.'''),
('Forces & gravitational interactions','''Newton's laws and force diagrams|A force diagram shows forces on one chosen object, not forces it exerts on others. Net force determines acceleration through Fnet = ma. Balanced forces mean zero acceleration, not necessarily zero velocity. Action-reaction forces act on different objects.|An object moving at constant velocity can have balanced forces.|A 2 kg object has net force 10 N. Its acceleration is…|5 m/s²|20 m/s²~0.2 m/s²~12 m/s²|Use a = Fnet/m = 10/2.|Net force~The vector sum of forces acting on an object.;Inertia~Resistance to a change in velocity associated with mass.
Friction and gravitation|Friction depends on surfaces and normal force in common models. Gravitational force follows an inverse-square relationship between point masses or suitable spherical masses. A field describes the force a test object would experience per relevant property.|Doubling separation reduces ideal gravitational force to one quarter.|Two masses keep the same values but their separation doubles. Gravitational force becomes…|One quarter|One half~Twice as large~Four times as large|The denominator contains distance squared: (2r)² = 4r².|Gravitational field~Gravitational force per unit mass.;Normal force~A contact force perpendicular to a surface.'''),
('Momentum & collisions','''Impulse and momentum|Momentum is mass times velocity. Impulse is force integrated over time and equals momentum change. Increasing collision duration can reduce average force for the same momentum change, which helps explain safety designs.|A helmet can reduce average impact force by lengthening stopping time.|For the same momentum change, doubling stopping time makes average force…|Half as large|Twice as large~Four times as large~Unchanged necessarily|Average force equals momentum change divided by elapsed time.|Momentum~The vector quantity mass times velocity.;Impulse~The change in momentum produced by force over time.
Conservation in interactions|A system's total momentum is conserved when external impulse is negligible. Kinetic energy is conserved in an elastic collision, but not every collision is elastic. Momentum transfer and energy transformation must be analyzed separately.|Two carts sticking together conserve total momentum in an isolated collision but transform kinetic energy.|Which quantity is conserved in an isolated inelastic collision?|Total momentum|Each object's velocity~Total kinetic energy necessarily~Each object's momentum|Internal interactions transfer momentum between objects without changing the system total.|Isolated system~A system with negligible relevant external interactions.;Inelastic collision~A collision in which total kinetic energy is not conserved.'''),
('Energy, power & thermal systems','''Work and mechanical energy|Work transfers energy through a force acting along displacement. Kinetic energy is mv²/2 and gravitational potential energy near Earth changes by mgΔh. Energy conservation accounts for all forms, including thermal energy when friction acts.|Doubling speed quadruples kinetic energy if mass stays constant.|A fixed-mass object's speed triples. Its kinetic energy becomes…|9 times as large|3 times as large~6 times as large~Unchanged|Kinetic energy depends on speed squared, so the factor is 3².|Work~Energy transfer by force acting through displacement.;Power~The rate of energy transfer.
Thermal transfer and efficiency|Heating can change temperature or phase. Thermal energy transfers from hotter to colder systems without additional intervention. Efficiency compares useful output with input; total energy remains conserved even when useful output is smaller. Devices often transfer some energy as dispersed heat.|A device giving 60 J useful output for 100 J input is 60% efficient.|A device has 200 J input and 50 J useful output. Efficiency is…|25%|75%~4%~250%|Efficiency = useful output/input × 100% = 50/200 × 100%.|Efficiency~Useful output energy divided by input energy.;Specific heat capacity~Energy per unit mass per degree of temperature increase.'''),
('Waves, sound & information','''Wave quantities and superposition|Waves transfer energy and information without net transport of the medium across the whole path. Speed equals frequency times wavelength. Amplitude relates to disturbance size. Superposition combines disturbances where waves overlap, producing interference patterns.|A wave with frequency 5 Hz and wavelength 2 m travels at 10 m/s.|For constant wave speed, increasing frequency decreases…|Wavelength|The speed necessarily~The source's existence~The number of cycles per second|v = fλ, so λ decreases when f rises at fixed v.|Frequency~Cycles per unit time.;Wavelength~Distance between corresponding points of successive cycles.
Reflection, refraction and communication|Reflection returns waves from a boundary; refraction changes direction when wave speed changes across media at suitable incidence. Diffraction spreads waves around openings. Doppler shifts arise from relative source-observer motion. Digital encoding supports reliable information transfer but has sampling limits.|Ultrasound echoes can locate boundaries; Doppler shifts can estimate flow speed.|When light enters another medium and changes speed and direction, the effect is…|Refraction|Nuclear decay~Conduction only~Polarization in every case|Refraction arises from changing propagation speed at a boundary, except direction need not change at normal incidence.|Refraction~A wave's change of propagation direction associated with a speed change.;Doppler effect~A frequency shift due to relative motion.'''),
('Electric forces & circuits','''Charge, fields and potential|Electric charge is conserved. Like charges repel and unlike charges attract. An electric field gives force per unit positive test charge; potential difference is energy transferred per charge. Field diagrams represent direction and relative strength, not literal threads.|A positive test charge accelerates in the electric-field direction if other forces are negligible.|Electric field direction is defined by force on a…|Positive test charge|Neutron only~Negative charge in every case~Massless wire|The conventional direction is the force on a positive test charge.|Electric field~Electric force per unit positive test charge.;Potential difference~Energy change per unit charge.
Current, resistance and circuits|Current is charge flow rate. For an ohmic component, V = IR. Series elements share current; parallel branches share potential difference. Conserve charge at junctions and track energy transfers around loops. Power can be calculated as VI.|A 6 V source across a 3 Ω ohmic resistor produces 2 A.|Two resistors are connected in parallel. What is the same across both?|Potential difference|Current necessarily~Resistance necessarily~Wire length necessarily|Parallel branches connect the same pair of circuit nodes, giving the same voltage.|Current~Charge passing a point per unit time.;Resistance~The ratio of potential difference to current for a specified operating condition.'''),
('Magnetism, light & atomic evidence','''Magnetic interactions and induction|Moving charges and currents produce magnetic effects. Magnetic forces can change charged-particle direction without doing work through that force alone. Changing magnetic flux can induce an electromotive force. Generators and motors exchange mechanical and electrical energy through interactions.|Moving a magnet relative to a coil can induce a changing electrical response.|A generator primarily converts…|Mechanical energy to electrical energy|Charge into mass in every case~Electrical energy to nothing~Gravity into chemical elements|Mechanical work drives electromagnetic induction to transfer energy electrically.|Induction~Generation of an electrical effect by changing magnetic flux.;Magnetic field~A representation of magnetic interactions.
Light, photons and nuclear evidence|Light displays wave phenomena and quantized interactions. Photon energy is hf. Spectra indicate discrete atomic energy changes; the photoelectric effect supports quantized energy transfer. Nuclear reactions involve different energy scales and particle changes than chemical reactions.|Increasing photon frequency increases its energy without requiring an increased photon count.|Which photon has greater energy?|The one with higher frequency|The one with longer wavelength always~All photons have equal energy~The one with lower frequency|E = hf with constant positive h, so energy increases with frequency.|Photon~A quantum of electromagnetic radiation.;Photoelectric effect~Emission of electrons from material through absorption of sufficiently energetic radiation.'''),
('Engineering, models & physical evidence','''Test models and analyze uncertainty|A model predicts relationships under stated assumptions. Compare predictions with data, residuals, and uncertainty rather than expecting exact agreement. Control variables and repeat measurements. A mismatch can reveal measurement limits, missing forces, or an unsuitable approximation.|A no-air-resistance model may fail for a light object falling through air.|Which is the best response to repeated disagreement with a prediction?|Check assumptions and measurement uncertainty|Discard all inconvenient data~Declare every model useless~Change units without recalculating|Evidence should guide revision of assumptions, procedures, or the model.|System boundary~The chosen limit separating a system from its surroundings.;Uncertainty~A limit on the precision or confidence of a result.
Design for performance and trade-offs|Engineering uses physics to meet criteria within constraints. Compare alternative designs with measurable tests and justify revisions. Safety, energy use, cost, and material availability can conflict. A successful design is supported by evidence rather than attractive presentation alone.|Compare helmet designs using peak force, stopping duration, mass, and repeated-test consistency.|Which is a measurable performance criterion for a helmet?|Maximum transmitted impact force|A fixed spending limit~The deadline alone~A supplier restriction|A force limit measures desired performance, whereas budget and deadline are constraints.|Criterion~A target used to judge a solution.;Constraint~A restriction limiting a design.''')])
course('global','Global History and Geography II','Humanities','Follow world history from about 1750 to today through sources, causation, and enduring issues.',[
('The world around 1750 & historical evidence','''Read sources in context|A source's origin, purpose, audience, and historical setting influence what it can show. A primary source comes from the period under study but is not automatically accurate or representative. Corroboration compares independent evidence rather than treating one voice as the whole society.|A ruler's decree can show official goals without proving those goals were achieved.|Which question best evaluates a source's historical context?|What events and conditions shaped its creation?|Is the font attractive?~Does it agree with my opinion?~Was it written in English?|Context situates the document within the circumstances that influenced its production.|Historical context~Relevant conditions surrounding an event or source.;Corroboration~Checking a claim against other evidence.
States, societies and geography in 1750|Around 1750, powerful states included the Ottoman, Qing, Mughal, and European monarchies. Authority depended on institutions, elite alliances, military power, and ideas. Geographic position affected trade and interaction; similar state labels do not imply identical systems.|Access to a major trade route could increase revenue while also attracting foreign competition.|Which factor most directly helps explain a port city's commercial importance?|Its location on trade networks|Its ruler's name alone~The absence of all exchange~Its distance from every waterway|Geographic access shapes opportunities for movement of goods and people.|Empire~A political system controlling multiple peoples or territories.;Geographic context~The spatial conditions influencing historical developments.'''),
('Enlightenment, revolution & nationalism','''Enlightenment and political change|Enlightenment thinkers debated rights, sovereignty, law, and limits on power. These ideas influenced revolutions but interacted with economic crises, social divisions, and existing traditions. Stated universal rights often coexisted with exclusions by race, class, gender, or legal status.|A claim that government needs consent challenges a ruler's unlimited authority.|Which principle most directly challenges absolute monarchy?|Government by consent|Divine right without limits~Hereditary privilege alone~Unaccountable rule|Consent locates political legitimacy in the governed rather than solely in inherited authority.|Enlightenment~An intellectual movement emphasizing reason and debate about society and authority.;Sovereignty~Ultimate political authority.
Revolutions and national identities|The American, French, Haitian, and Latin American revolutions differed in causes and outcomes. Nationalism could support independence, unification, or exclusion. Evaluate which groups gained rights and whether political change altered economic and social relationships.|Haiti's revolution challenged both colonial rule and slavery, unlike revolutions that preserved enslavement.|A movement seeking to unify people under a shared national identity most directly reflects…|Nationalism|Isolation from all politics~Feudal obligation alone~Absolute religious uniformity by definition|Nationalism links political belonging and claims to a shared nation.|Nationalism~Identification with a nation and support for its political claims.;Revolution~A major transformation in political or social order.'''),
('Industrialization & social transformation','''Factories, technology and labor|Industrialization shifted production through mechanization, factories, new energy sources, and changing labor organization. Resources, capital, transport, and institutions contributed to early growth. Urbanization created opportunities and serious health and labor problems; outcomes varied by group.|Steam power and transport networks helped connect factories with markets and resources.|Which change is most associated with industrialization?|Expansion of mechanized factory production|Universal return to subsistence farming~Elimination of urban growth~An end to fossil-fuel use|Industrialization reorganized production around machinery, energy systems, and concentrated workplaces.|Industrialization~Growth of mechanized production and associated economic change.;Urbanization~An increasing concentration of population in towns and cities.
Economic ideas and reform|Industrial change encouraged debates over markets, ownership, inequality, and government action. Capitalism, socialism, and Marxism propose different relationships among labor, property, and power. Workers organized unions and reform movements; reforms emerged through conflict and varied across states.|Factory safety laws represent government intervention addressing conditions not resolved by owners alone.|A labor union primarily seeks collective influence over…|Work conditions and compensation|Astronomical observations~Royal succession only~All international borders|Collective organization can strengthen workers' bargaining power.|Labor union~An organization representing workers' shared interests.;Socialism~A family of ideas favoring social or collective control of major economic resources.'''),
('Imperialism & resistance','''Motives and methods of imperialism|Industrial powers expanded control for resources, markets, strategic access, and prestige. Racial ideologies and claims of civilizing missions justified domination but did not make it consensual. Formal colonies and informal economic influence used different mechanisms.|Control of a canal could provide strategic leverage even beyond direct resource extraction.|Which economic motive encouraged nineteenth-century imperial expansion?|Access to raw materials and markets|Eliminating all overseas trade~Ending industrial demand~Making all colonies equal partners automatically|Imperial control could secure inputs and markets for industrial economies.|Imperialism~Expansion of power over other peoples or territories.;Colony~A territory under the political control of an external power.
Resistance and unequal power|Colonized peoples responded through armed resistance, diplomacy, reform, and cultural strategies. Responses in India, China, Africa, and elsewhere differed. Analyze local agency alongside military and economic inequalities. A failed uprising can still shape later nationalist movements.|The Indian Rebellion of 1857 and Chinese resistance movements reflected distinct grievances under outside pressure.|Why compare resistance movements in different colonies?|To identify shared pressures and differing local responses|To prove all responses were identical~To remove historical context~To assume colonized peoples had no agency|Comparison needs both similarities and differences, grounded in each setting.|Resistance~Action opposing imposed authority or control.;Agency~People's capacity to act within historical conditions.'''),
('World War I & revolutionary change','''Causes and consequences of World War I|Militarism, alliances, imperial competition, and nationalism increased European tensions. The 1914 assassination triggered a crisis within these conditions rather than being a complete explanation alone. Total war mobilized economies and civilians, changing social relationships and state power.|An alliance system could turn a regional conflict into a wider war.|Which distinction best separates an immediate trigger from a long-term cause?|The assassination versus prewar alliance tensions|Two names for the same event always~A treaty versus its spelling~An opinion versus every fact|Triggers begin a crisis; underlying conditions help explain why it escalates.|Militarism~Emphasis on military power and readiness in policy.;Total war~Mobilization affecting much of a society's people and resources.
Russian Revolution and peace settlements|War pressures contributed to revolution in Russia and the Bolshevik rise. Peace settlements redrew borders, dismantled empires, and created grievances. Self-determination was applied unevenly. Evaluate treaty effects alongside economic disruption and political choices rather than assuming later war was inevitable.|A new border could create a state while leaving minority populations inside it.|A central challenge to applying national self-determination was…|Mixed populations within proposed borders|The absence of any national identities~Universal agreement on all borders~An end to imperial interests|Ethnic and linguistic populations did not fit neatly into every proposed national boundary.|Self-determination~The claim that a people should decide its political status.;Bolsheviks~The revolutionary group that took power in Russia in 1917.'''),
('Interwar crisis, World War II & genocide','''Depression and authoritarianism|The Great Depression disrupted trade, employment, and confidence in institutions. Fascist and other authoritarian movements mobilized nationalism, coercion, and control of information. Economic crisis can create opportunities for such movements, but political outcomes also depend on institutions and choices.|Censorship can weaken opposition by limiting public access to competing accounts.|Which practice most directly weakens democratic accountability?|Suppressing independent political opposition|Holding competitive elections~Protecting a free press~Allowing peaceful criticism|Accountability depends on meaningful opportunities to question and replace leaders.|Authoritarianism~Government concentrating power while limiting political competition and liberties.;Fascism~An authoritarian ultranationalist political ideology and movement.
War, Holocaust and human responsibility|World War II involved aggressive expansion, occupation, resistance, and mass civilian harm. The Holocaust was the Nazi genocide of Europe's Jews; Nazis also persecuted and murdered other groups. Study policy, institutions, prejudice, and individual choices using reliable evidence, not flattened comparisons.|Nuremberg trials addressed responsibility for major wartime crimes rather than treating state orders as automatic immunity.|What did postwar war-crimes trials challenge?|The claim that officials cannot be held accountable for atrocities|The existence of civilian protections~All historical documentation~Every form of international cooperation|The trials helped establish individual accountability for major international crimes.|Genocide~Acts intended to destroy, in whole or part, a protected national, ethnic, racial, or religious group.;Holocaust~The Nazi-led genocide of six million European Jews.'''),
('Cold War & competing systems','''Bipolar competition and proxy conflicts|The Cold War involved ideological, military, economic, and diplomatic competition, especially between the United States and Soviet Union. Nuclear deterrence and alliances shaped decisions. Proxy wars had local causes and consequences and should not be reduced to outside rivalry alone.|The Korean conflict reflected local division as well as international Cold War involvement.|A proxy conflict involves…|Rivals supporting different sides in another conflict|Only direct war between the two largest rivals~No local interests~A conflict without political effects|External support connects broader rivalry with a conflict that also has its own context.|Cold War~Sustained rivalry below direct full-scale war between the main powers.;Deterrence~Discouraging action through the threat of costly consequences.
Reform, dissent and the Cold War's end|Dissent, economic pressures, reform efforts, and nationalist movements contributed to changes in communist states. The Soviet Union's collapse reshaped borders and power relationships. A single leader or one event alone is not a sufficient explanation of these varied processes.|Political openness can expose grievances and enable movements beyond reformers' original goals.|Which is a reasonable historical explanation for the Soviet collapse?|Multiple political, economic, and nationalist pressures|One cause explaining every society~A change with no prior conditions~Proof that all reforms have identical outcomes|Historical explanation connects interacting causes rather than assuming a single universal mechanism.|Dissent~Expression of opposition to prevailing authority or policy.;Bipolarity~An international system dominated by two major power centers.'''),
('Decolonization & nation-building','''Independence movements|Decolonization accelerated after World War II through negotiation, mass mobilization, armed struggles, and international pressure. Leaders such as Gandhi, Nkrumah, and others pursued distinct strategies in specific settings. Independence did not automatically resolve unequal economies or internal divisions.|Partition of British India created independent states and also mass displacement and violence.|Which conclusion best describes decolonization?|Political independence often left major social and economic challenges|Every colony gained independence peacefully~Independence erased every border dispute~All movements used one strategy|Ending formal rule did not remove all inherited structures or competing claims.|Decolonization~The ending of colonial political control.;Partition~Division of a territory into separate political units.
Postcolonial states and development|New states faced choices about institutions, development, identity, and foreign alignment. Nonalignment sought room outside Cold War blocs but did not imply no international relationships. Compare development indicators carefully; averages can hide regional or group inequality.|A country's rising average income may coexist with unequal access to healthcare or schooling.|Why should development be measured with several indicators?|Income alone can hide differences in wellbeing and distribution|Every indicator measures exactly the same thing~No evidence can describe development~Only military size matters|Health, education, income, and distribution reveal different aspects of living conditions.|Nonalignment~Avoiding formal alignment with the main Cold War blocs.;Development indicator~A measure used to evaluate economic or social conditions.'''),
('Human rights & social movements','''Rights claims and international institutions|Postwar human-rights frameworks expressed claims to dignity and protection across borders. International institutions can document abuses and coordinate action but face enforcement and sovereignty limits. Compare principles with implementation, including selective or unequal responses.|A declaration can establish a norm without guaranteeing compliance by every state.|Why can a human-rights declaration matter despite weak enforcement?|It provides standards for advocacy and accountability|It automatically prevents every abuse~It replaces all domestic laws immediately~It eliminates political disagreement|Shared standards can support criticism and mobilization even when enforcement is incomplete.|Human rights~Rights claimed for people by virtue of being human.;Sovereignty~A state's authority over its territory and political affairs.
Movements against exclusion|Movements against apartheid, discrimination, and gender inequality used protest, organization, legal challenges, and international pressure. Evaluate who participated, whose voices were excluded, and how institutions changed. Formal legal change and everyday equality are related but different outcomes.|Ending apartheid laws in South Africa was a major transformation without instantly removing economic inequality.|Which evidence best shows an institutional change?|A discriminatory law being repealed|A rumor about one individual~One observer's preference~A change in a slogan's color|Institutional change alters rules or durable practices, not merely personal impressions.|Apartheid~South Africa's former state system of racial segregation and domination.;Social movement~Organized collective action seeking or resisting social change.'''),
('Globalization, enduring issues & writing','''Interdependence and global challenges|Trade, migration, communication, and environmental processes connect societies. Globalization creates benefits and unequal vulnerabilities. Analyze climate change, resource use, migration, and technology through evidence across regions and times, distinguishing a pattern from an unsupported generalization.|A disruption in one manufacturing region can affect supplies across several countries.|Which example best illustrates economic interdependence?|A product assembled from components made in multiple countries|A country with no external exchange by definition~One person's private diary~An isolated measurement of rainfall|Cross-border production links economic activities and exposes participants to shared disruptions.|Globalization~Intensification of connections across world regions.;Interdependence~Mutual reliance among people or systems.
Constructed responses and enduring issues|A strong response identifies context, uses document evidence accurately, and explains relationships. An enduring-issues essay defines an issue, supports it with relevant documents and outside knowledge, and traces persistence or change. Listing events without analysis does not establish endurance.|For conflict, connect examples from different periods and explain why the issue persisted or changed.|What most strengthens an enduring-issues argument?|Evidence across times with explanation of continuity or change|A list of unrelated dates~Copying every document~A claim with no examples|Endurance must be demonstrated through relationships across cases, not merely asserted.|Enduring issue~A challenge or condition persisting or recurring across time.;Causation~A reasoned relationship explaining how one condition contributes to another.''')])

course('us','U.S. History and Government','Humanities','Trace U.S. history, constitutional debates, civic change, and evidence-based historical writing.',[
('Colonial foundations','''Geography, colonization and diversity|Indigenous societies had diverse political, economic, and cultural systems before European colonization. Colonies developed through interactions among land, labor, trade, environment, and power. European expansion displaced Indigenous peoples and depended in many places on enslaved labor.|Different climates and labor systems contributed to differences between New England and plantation regions.|Which explanation best accounts for regional colonial economic differences?|Environment, labor, markets, and institutions interacted|Every colony had identical resources~Only latitude determined every outcome~No region participated in trade|Geography matters alongside human institutions and choices; it is not a complete deterministic explanation.|Colonization~Establishment of control and settlement by an external power.;Enslavement~The coercive treatment of people as property.
Government, economy and resistance|Colonial governments combined local institutions with imperial authority. Mercantilist policies linked colonies to imperial trade interests. Colonists' experiences with representation and rights influenced later resistance, but political participation excluded many people.|A colonial assembly could give some settlers local political experience without universal democracy.|A major purpose of mercantilist trade restrictions was to…|Benefit the imperial economy|Guarantee equal political rights to everyone~End every overseas market~Create colonial independence|Imperial trade rules sought revenue and advantage for the mother country.|Mercantilism~An economic approach linking regulated trade to state power.;Representative assembly~A body whose members act on behalf of selected constituents.'''),
('Revolution & constitutional foundations','''Revolution and competing visions|Disputes over taxation, representation, authority, and rights contributed to revolution. The Declaration articulated natural-rights and consent arguments, yet practice contradicted universal claims. Analyze perspectives of Patriots, Loyalists, Indigenous nations, enslaved people, and other groups rather than assuming one unified experience.|A demand for consent challenged imperial policies without immediately extending voting rights to everyone.|The Declaration's argument about consent most directly concerns…|The source of legitimate government authority|The formula for currency value~An end to all inequality already achieved~The structure of chemical matter|Consent links political legitimacy to the governed rather than solely to inherited rule.|Natural rights~Rights understood as belonging to people independently of government.;Consent of the governed~The principle that legitimate authority depends on the people's agreement.
The Constitution and federalism|The Constitution created a stronger national government than the Articles of Confederation while dividing authority. Separation of powers and checks and balances distribute institutional control. Federalism allocates power between national and state governments. Ratification debates produced continuing arguments about rights and representation.|A presidential veto and congressional override illustrate checks and balances.|Which arrangement is federalism?|Division of power between national and state governments|One branch performing every function~No written rules~All authority held by local clubs|Federalism concerns levels of government, distinct from separation among branches.|Federalism~A constitutional division of power between national and state governments.;Checks and balances~Institutional powers limiting other branches.'''),
('Expansion, sectionalism & Civil War','''Expansion and conflict|Territorial growth intensified disputes over slavery, Indigenous land, national authority, and economic development. Manifest Destiny justified expansion for many supporters but concealed coercion and competing rights. Evaluate who benefited, who was displaced, and how new territories changed political conflict.|Debates about slavery in new territories affected the balance of power in Congress.|Why did territorial expansion intensify sectional conflict?|It raised disputes over slavery's extension and political power|It removed all economic differences~It ended Indigenous displacement~It made congressional representation irrelevant|Territorial decisions connected slavery and representation to national political power.|Sectionalism~Political loyalty and interests organized around a region.;Manifest Destiny~A belief used to justify U.S. continental expansion.
Civil War and emancipation|Slavery was central to secession and the Civil War, alongside disputes about federal power in protecting that institution. Emancipation developed through enslaved people's actions, military events, policy, and constitutional change. Distinguish a wartime proclamation's reach from a constitutional amendment.|The Thirteenth Amendment abolished slavery, except as punishment for crime, throughout the United States.|Which measure constitutionally abolished slavery in the United States?|Thirteenth Amendment|Emancipation Proclamation alone~Missouri Compromise~Articles of Confederation|The proclamation had specific wartime limits; the amendment changed the Constitution nationally.|Emancipation~Release from enslavement.;Secession~Withdrawal from a political union.'''),
('Reconstruction & its limits','''Reconstruction amendments and citizenship|The Thirteenth, Fourteenth, and Fifteenth Amendments reshaped slavery, citizenship, equal protection, and voting protections. Reconstruction involved disputes over federal enforcement, state power, labor, and representation. Constitutional text and practical access to rights did not always match.|The Fourteenth Amendment established national citizenship and equal-protection guarantees.|Which amendment contains equal protection of the laws?|Fourteenth|First~Tenth~Eighteenth|The Fourteenth Amendment addresses citizenship, due process, and equal protection.|Equal protection~A constitutional guarantee concerning government treatment under law.;Reconstruction~The post-Civil War era of rebuilding and redefining political and civil rights.
Resistance and long-term consequences|White supremacist violence, discriminatory laws, and weakened federal enforcement undermined Reconstruction's promises. Segregation and disfranchisement developed through institutions, not merely personal attitudes. Black communities continued organizing, building institutions, and claiming constitutional rights.|Poll taxes and literacy tests were used to suppress voting despite constitutional protections.|Which evidence shows the gap between formal rights and actual access?|Legal voting protections alongside systematic voter suppression|An amendment and its official number~A map legend alone~One unrelated weather report|Rights on paper can be limited through violence, administration, and discriminatory practices.|Disfranchisement~Denial or removal of effective voting rights.;Segregation~Institutional separation of people, often by race.'''),
('Industrialization, immigration & reform','''Industry, labor and immigration|Industrial growth expanded corporations, transportation, cities, and wage labor. Immigration supplied workers and reshaped communities, while exclusionary policies restricted particular groups. Labor conflicts reflected disputes about wages, hours, safety, and power. Benefits of growth were distributed unequally.|The Chinese Exclusion Act illustrates how immigration policy could target a specific population.|A major issue motivating labor organizing was…|Unsafe conditions and unequal bargaining power|An end to all factory employment~Universal ownership equality already achieved~Absence of urban work|Workers organized to influence the terms and safety of employment.|Corporation~A legally constituted business entity separate from its owners.;Collective bargaining~Negotiation between organized workers and employers.
Populism and Progressive reform|Populists and Progressives addressed economic power and democratic participation in different contexts. Reforms included regulation, antitrust action, consumer protection, and suffrage expansion. Evaluate reformers' exclusions and contradictions alongside achievements.|Investigative reporting helped build support for food and drug regulation.|Which action most directly reflects Progressive regulation?|Government standards for food safety|Ending every election~Removing all corporate law~A ban on public investigation|Progressive reform often used government power to address problems associated with industrialization.|Progressivism~A reform movement seeking responses to industrial and political problems.;Muckraking~Investigative reporting exposing abuses or corruption.'''),
('U.S. power & World War I','''Imperial expansion and debate|By the late nineteenth century, U.S. policymakers debated overseas expansion, trade, strategic bases, and national ideals. The Spanish-American War and acquisition of territories raised questions about consent and constitutional rights. Economic motives were important but not the only explanation.|Anti-imperialists argued that ruling others without consent conflicted with republican principles.|Which debate accompanied U.S. overseas expansion?|Whether imperial rule conflicted with self-government|Whether the Civil War had occurred~Whether all territories were empty~Whether trade had ceased|Expansion forced arguments about the relationship between power, rights, and national principles.|Imperialism~Extending political or economic control beyond national borders.;Self-government~Political rule exercised by a people through their own institutions.
World War I and domestic tensions|U.S. entry into World War I reflected security, economic, diplomatic, and ideological concerns. Wartime mobilization affected labor, migration, and civil liberties. Wilson's international vision met opposition over sovereignty and obligations, contributing to U.S. rejection of League membership.|Wartime speech restrictions raised constitutional questions about dissent and national security.|Opposition to joining the League of Nations often focused on…|Fear of binding international commitments|A plan to abolish every state~The absence of any treaty~A desire to end all commerce|Critics worried collective-security commitments could limit independent U.S. decisions.|Collective security~Cooperation against threats to international peace.;Civil liberty~A freedom protected against government intrusion.'''),
('Prosperity, Depression & New Deal','''The 1920s and the crash|The 1920s combined consumer growth, cultural change, inequality, racial violence, and political conflict. Credit expansion and speculation contributed to financial vulnerability, while the Depression had multiple causes. The stock-market crash was significant but not a complete explanation.|Farm distress and unequal income distribution existed before the 1929 crash.|Which interpretation best explains the Great Depression?|Interacting financial, production, demand, and policy problems|One stock sale caused every consequence~Universal prosperity continued unchanged~No international connections mattered|Complex economic crises require analysis of several interacting mechanisms.|Speculation~Risk-taking based on expected price changes.;Economic depression~A severe, sustained downturn in economic activity.
New Deal and government's role|New Deal programs pursued relief, recovery, and reform through expanded federal action. They faced constitutional challenges, political opposition, and unequal implementation. Social Security and financial regulation changed institutions, while debates over government responsibility continued.|Banking regulation sought to reduce financial instability rather than merely provide temporary emergency aid.|A lasting banking regulation is best classified as…|Reform|A declaration of independence~Only immediate relief~A military alliance|Reform changes institutions or rules to reduce future problems.|Relief~Immediate assistance to people in crisis.;Reform~Structural change intended to address recurring problems.'''),
('World War II & its domestic effects','''Mobilization and global strategy|World War II mobilized industry, labor, science, and military power. U.S. strategy involved alliances and competing priorities across theaters. War production accelerated economic changes and opened some opportunities while discrimination remained.|Defense employment drew workers to industrial centers and changed migration patterns.|Which change directly supported U.S. wartime mobilization?|Conversion of factories to military production|Elimination of all federal spending~Ending every alliance~Closing all transport networks|Industrial conversion increased the supply of war materials.|Mobilization~Organization of resources and people for a major collective effort.;Alliance~An agreement to cooperate toward shared security or political goals.
Rights, incarceration and accountability|The wartime incarceration of Japanese Americans involved grave violations of civil liberties. Black Americans pursued the Double V campaign against fascism and domestic racism. Compare wartime claims of freedom with unequal treatment, using documents and later reassessment as evidence.|A claim of national security can be analyzed critically against evidence and constitutional protections.|Which question best evaluates a wartime restriction on rights?|Was the restriction justified by evidence and consistent with constitutional protections?|Did every official use the same slogan?~Was criticism inconvenient?~Did the policy have a short name?|Historical and civic analysis requires evidence, proportionality, and attention to rights.|Civil rights~Legal protections of equal participation and treatment.;Double V~A campaign linking victory abroad with victory over racism at home.'''),
('Cold War foreign policy','''Containment and conflict|Containment aimed to limit communist expansion through economic aid, alliances, and military action. The Truman Doctrine, Marshall Plan, NATO, and conflicts in Korea and Vietnam reflected different tools and contexts. Policy goals and actual consequences were not always the same.|Economic reconstruction aid could pursue political stability as well as material recovery.|The Marshall Plan primarily used which tool?|Economic assistance|Annexation of Western Europe~A constitutional amendment~Domestic voting restrictions|Aid supported European recovery and Cold War strategic goals.|Containment~A strategy to limit the expansion of a rival power or ideology.;NATO~A collective-defense alliance formed in 1949.
Nuclear crises, dissent and diplomacy|Nuclear weapons increased the stakes of confrontation. The Cuban Missile Crisis involved deterrence and negotiation; later arms-control efforts attempted to reduce risks. The Vietnam War generated major domestic debate about credibility, limits of power, and government accountability.|Negotiated removal of missiles illustrates diplomacy operating alongside military pressure.|What makes nuclear deterrence distinctive?|Threats involve potentially catastrophic retaliation|It eliminates every possibility of conflict~It requires no communication~It guarantees cooperation|Deterrence depends on expectations of severe consequences, with uncertainty and escalation risks.|Arms control~Agreements limiting weapons or their use.;Détente~A period or policy of reduced tension between rivals.'''),
('Domestic change & civil-rights movements','''Civil rights and federal action|The modern civil-rights movement combined local organization, litigation, direct action, and federal legislation. Brown, the Civil Rights Act of 1964, and Voting Rights Act of 1965 addressed different institutions. Legal gains did not automatically remove economic or social inequality.|A federal ban on discriminatory voting practices targets access to political participation.|The Voting Rights Act of 1965 most directly addressed…|Racial discrimination in voting|The creation of the presidency~The end of all foreign wars~The founding of the colonies|The act targeted barriers and discriminatory practices limiting voting rights.|Direct action~Organized public action seeking change outside ordinary institutional channels.;Judicial review~Court evaluation of government action's constitutionality.
Expanding rights and political debates|Women, Native Americans, Latino communities, LGBTQ communities, disability-rights advocates, and others pursued varied claims. Debates over federal power, privacy, economic policy, and equality reshaped politics. Compare goals and strategies without treating distinct movements as interchangeable.|Accessible public facilities can change participation opportunities through institutional design.|Which evidence best demonstrates increased accessibility?|Removal of physical and institutional barriers|A slogan without policy change~A guarantee that every inequality vanished~A single unrelated opinion poll|Accessibility concerns whether people can meaningfully use spaces, services, and institutions.|Accessibility~The ability to use and participate in spaces or institutions.;Grassroots organizing~Collective action built through local participation.'''),
('A globalizing nation & civic literacy','''Economic change, technology and security|Late twentieth- and early twenty-first-century U.S. history includes globalization, deindustrialization, technological change, immigration debates, and responses to terrorism. Policy choices involve trade-offs among security, rights, economic interests, and international relationships. Evaluate claims using evidence and context.|A factory closure can reflect technology, trade, corporate decisions, and policy rather than one universal cause.|Which is the strongest explanation of economic change?|A claim linking several relevant mechanisms to evidence|A single slogan explaining all regions~An unsupported assumption~A claim ignoring chronology|A historical explanation must connect evidence to plausible mechanisms across time.|Deindustrialization~Decline in industrial employment or production within a region.;Globalization~Increasing connections in production, trade, communication, and migration.
Civic literacy and historical essays|Short essays establish context and explain a relationship or evaluate reliability. Civic-literacy essays analyze a constitutional or civic issue, efforts to address it, and impacts. Use documents and relevant outside knowledge; distinguish source evidence from your explanation of its significance.|For immigration restriction, explain circumstances, legislative action, affected groups, and later responses.|What most improves a civic-literacy essay?|Explaining how evidence supports an argument about actions and impacts|Copying documents without analysis~Listing dates with no claim~Ignoring counterevidence|An essay needs a defensible argument and reasoning, not only collected facts.|Civic literacy~Knowledge and reasoning about rights, institutions, participation, and public issues.;Historical argument~A supported interpretation explaining historical developments.''')])

course('ela','English Language Arts','Humanities','Read closely, analyze craft, build arguments, and revise evidence-based writing.',[
('Close reading & textual evidence','''Literal meaning and inference|Close reading distinguishes what a text states from what readers infer. A sound inference connects details through reasoning and remains open to alternatives. Select evidence that directly supports the specific claim rather than a quotation that only mentions the topic.|Original practice: The character reread the unanswered letter and placed it beside the phone. This supports concern about communication, not certainty about the letter's contents.|Which makes an inference strongest?|Several relevant details connected by reasoning|A guess based only on preference~A statement contradicting the passage~The longest available quotation|An inference needs textual support and an explained connection, not merely plausibility.|Inference~A conclusion supported by evidence and reasoning beyond explicit statements.;Textual evidence~Specific details from a text supporting an interpretation.
Central ideas and objective summaries|A central idea describes a developed meaning, while a topic names a subject. Trace how details contribute to that idea across a text. An objective summary represents the text's key points accurately without inserting personal evaluation or minor details.|Topic: friendship. Possible central idea: Trust develops through repeated acts of reliability.|Which is a central idea rather than only a topic?|Trust grows through consistent action|Trust~Friends~A letter|A central idea states a developed insight about the topic, rather than only naming it.|Central idea~A major meaning developed by a text.;Objective summary~An accurate account of key content without personal judgment.'''),
('Language, diction & tone','''Vocabulary in context|Word meaning depends on context, connotation, syntax, and use. A familiar word can have an unfamiliar sense. Try a meaning in the sentence and check it against surrounding details rather than choosing the first dictionary definition.|Original practice: After three failed attempts, she offered a measured reply. Measured suggests restraint, not a ruler.|In a tense discussion, a measured response most likely means…|A carefully restrained response|A response expressed in centimeters~A response with no words~A physically heavy response|Context points to careful control rather than literal measurement.|Connotation~Associations or emotional suggestions carried by a word.;Diction~An author's word choices.
Tone and figurative language|Tone is an attitude conveyed through language, not the reader's mood alone. Imagery appeals to sensory experience; metaphor relates unlike things without literal identity. Explain how a choice shapes meaning rather than simply labeling a device.|Original practice: The deadline stalked him suggests pressure by presenting time as a pursuing creature.|What does personification do?|Gives human qualities to a nonhuman thing|Always proves a factual claim~Eliminates imagery~Makes every sentence literal|Personification assigns human action or qualities to nonhuman subjects to shape an impression.|Tone~An attitude conveyed by a text's language.;Personification~Attributing human qualities to nonhuman things.'''),
('Literary perspective & development','''Narration, characterization and conflict|A narrator's knowledge and perspective shape access to events. Distinguish narrator from author. Characterization emerges from actions, dialogue, thought, and others' reactions. Conflict may be internal or external; a character's change needs evidence across the text.|A first-person narrator can report personal thoughts without knowing every other character's thoughts.|A first-person account is limited mainly by…|The narrator's perspective and knowledge|The absence of any characterization~A rule that it must be factual~The author's inability to use dialogue|Narration controls access to information; first person does not automatically provide omniscience or truth.|Narrator~The voice presenting a narrative.;Characterization~How a text develops a character's qualities.
Theme, setting and symbolism|A theme is an insight developed through a text, not a one-word label or an unsupported moral. Setting can shape choices and conflict. A symbol gains significance through textual patterns; ordinary objects do not automatically have a fixed universal meaning.|An repeatedly locked gate may support a theme about exclusion when characters' experiences reinforce that meaning.|What is the best basis for interpreting a symbol?|Its repeated role and connections within the text|A universal meaning assigned without context~The object's price alone~The reader's favorite color|Symbolic significance must be supported by patterns and relationships in the work.|Theme~An insight about experience developed through a text.;Symbol~An element carrying meaning beyond its literal role.'''),
('Structure, genre & craft','''Analyze structure and pacing|Authors organize events and ideas to guide attention and create effects. Flashbacks can supply context; withheld information can create suspense. Paragraph order and transitions shape an argument's logic. Explain what a structural choice accomplishes in the specific text.|Beginning with an outcome before narrating its causes can focus attention on how that outcome developed.|Why might an author insert a flashback?|To reveal earlier context affecting present events|To guarantee every narrator is reliable~To remove chronological relationships entirely~To replace all characterization|A flashback changes time order to develop understanding of current action or character.|Structure~The organization of a text's parts.;Pacing~The rate and distribution of narrative development.
Compare texts and perspectives|Comparison needs a shared basis and precise differences. Two texts on one topic may differ in purpose, evidence, genre, or perspective. Separate disagreement over facts from different interpretations or emphases. Cite both texts rather than making unsupported general claims.|An editorial and research report may address the same policy but use different conventions and purposes.|A strong comparison should…|Use evidence from both texts on a shared question|Summarize only the first text~Assume all genres work alike~List unrelated quotations|A meaningful comparison connects specific similarities and differences to the common focus.|Perspective~A position or viewpoint shaping interpretation.;Genre~A category of text with recognizable purposes and conventions.'''),
('Evaluate arguments & sources','''Claims, reasons and evidence|An argument connects a claim to reasons and relevant evidence. Evaluate whether the evidence is sufficient, credible, and logically connected. Statistics can be accurate yet irrelevant. An unsupported generalization exceeds what the evidence can establish.|A survey of one classroom cannot alone establish what all state students prefer.|Which evidence best supports a statewide student-preference claim?|A well-designed representative sample|A survey of three friends~One advertisement~An anonymous unsupported comment|Representativeness and sound methods matter for generalizing to a broader population.|Claim~A position or assertion requiring support.;Reasoning~The explanation connecting evidence to a claim.
Rhetoric and counterarguments|Rhetorical choices appeal to audiences and shape persuasion. Evaluate appeals using evidence and logic rather than assuming emotion always invalidates an argument. A fair counterargument presents a relevant opposing position before answering it; a straw man distorts that position.|Addressing a real implementation cost is stronger than pretending opponents dislike all progress.|What distinguishes a fair counterargument?|Accurate treatment of a relevant opposing view|Ridicule replacing evidence~An invented weak opponent~Repetition of the main claim only|A counterargument strengthens analysis when the opposing view is represented accurately and answered.|Counterargument~A relevant position opposing or qualifying a claim.;Rhetoric~Strategic use of language to influence an audience.'''),
('Evidence-based argument writing','''Plan and synthesize an argument|Define a defensible position responding to the task. Group sources by reasons and relationships rather than writing one disconnected paragraph per source. Synthesis combines evidence to support analysis while preserving each source's meaning.|One paragraph can connect two sources that offer different evidence about the same benefit.|Which plan best supports synthesis?|Organize paragraphs around reasons using related sources|Copy one source per paragraph without a claim~List quotations in source order only~Omit all opposing evidence|Reason-centered organization helps evidence contribute to one developed argument.|Thesis~A central defensible claim guiding an essay.;Synthesis~Combining evidence or ideas into a coherent understanding.
Develop, cite and qualify|Introduce evidence, integrate it accurately, and explain its significance. Cite sources as the task requires. Qualify claims when evidence has limits; overstatement weakens credibility. A conclusion should follow the developed reasoning rather than introduce an unrelated new claim.|Instead of This solves everything, write The evidence suggests this reduces one identified barrier under these conditions.|What should follow a relevant quotation?|Explanation of how it supports the paragraph's claim|An unrelated quotation~A claim that length proves quality~No interpretation under any circumstances|Evidence needs reasoning to show its significance in the writer's argument.|Qualification~A limitation or condition narrowing a claim.;Citation~Identification of the source of borrowed evidence or ideas.'''),
('Text-analysis response','''Identify an idea and a strategy|A text-analysis response explains how an author uses a writing strategy to develop a central idea. Name a specific strategy and a defensible idea, then use precise evidence. Device spotting without a meaning-based explanation does not complete the task.|An analysis might explain how contrasting images develop an idea about change and continuity.|Which thesis best fits text analysis?|The contrasting images develop the idea that progress can involve loss|The passage has several sentences~I liked the ending~The author uses words|A successful analysis connects a concrete strategy to a developed meaning.|Writing strategy~A craft choice used to develop meaning or effect.;Analysis~Explanation of how parts and relationships contribute to a whole.
Explain effects with precise evidence|Choose brief, relevant details and explain the effect of the author's choices. Avoid retelling the entire plot. Distinguish the meaning of an example from the larger idea it helps develop. Revise to remove unsupported statements about author intent.|A repeated image can accumulate significance as the surrounding situation changes.|Which revision most improves a device-only statement?|Explain how the device develops the central idea|Add more device names without examples~Replace evidence with praise~Retell every event|The task concerns the relationship between craft and meaning, supported by specific details.|Commentary~A writer's explanation of evidence and its significance.;Repetition~Recurring language or elements used to create an effect.'''),
('Revision, conventions & exam practice','''Revise coherence and conventions|Revision improves reasoning, organization, and development; editing improves conventions and precision. Check pronoun references, verb agreement, sentence boundaries, and punctuation. A longer sentence is not automatically more sophisticated, and correctness alone cannot replace a developed argument.|Two independent clauses can be joined by a semicolon, or by a comma plus coordinating conjunction.|Which correctly joins two independent clauses?|The evidence is limited; the claim needs qualification.|The evidence is limited, the claim needs qualification.~Because the evidence is limited.~The evidence limited the claim need.|A semicolon can join related independent clauses; a comma alone creates a comma splice.|Revision~Reconsidering content, reasoning, and organization.;Comma splice~Joining independent clauses with only a comma.
Read the task and evaluate your response|Distinguish reading questions, source-based argument, and text analysis. Budget time for reading, planning, writing, and checking. Use official rubrics to self-evaluate evidence, reasoning, organization, and conventions. A practice platform's feedback is not an official Regents essay score.|Before revising, compare your response with the exact task and rubric descriptors.|Which is the best final check for an essay?|Whether it answers the task with supported reasoning|Whether it uses the longest words~Whether it reaches an arbitrary quotation count~Whether every paragraph is identical in length|Task alignment, evidence, and reasoning matter more than superficial length or vocabulary signals.|Rubric~Criteria and performance descriptions used to evaluate work.;Task alignment~How directly a response fulfills the assigned purpose.''')])
# Scope-audit additions: explicit instruction instead of treating a broad mention as coverage.
def extra(id,unit,rows,new_after=None):
 course('_temporary','','','',[(unit,rows)])
 additions=COURSES.pop()['units'][0]
 target=next(c for c in COURSES if c['id']==id)
 if new_after is None:next(u for u in target['units']if u['title']==unit)['lessons'].extend(additions['lessons'])
 else:target['units'].insert(new_after,additions)
extra('algebra1','Functions, sequences & transformations','''Absolute value and piecewise rules|Absolute value measures distance from zero. A piecewise function uses different rules on specified input intervals. Check interval endpoints carefully; the function must assign one output per input. Step and absolute-value graphs model different types of change.|A fee is $4 for x ≤ 2 and $4 + 2(x − 2) for x > 2; the correct rule depends on x.|What is absolute value of −7?|7|−7~0~49|Distance from zero is nonnegative, so the absolute value of −7 is 7.|Absolute value~Distance from zero on the number line.;Piecewise function~A function defined by different rules on specified parts of its domain.''')
extra('algebra1','Polynomials & factoring','''Exponent rules and structure|For common bases, multiplying powers adds exponents and dividing subtracts them when denominators are nonzero. Raising a power to a power multiplies exponents. Zero and negative exponents describe relationships preserving these rules, not new arithmetic exceptions.|For x ≠ 0, x⁵/x² = x³ and x⁻² = 1/x².|Simplify x³ · x⁴.|x⁷|x¹²~2x⁷~x|There are seven total factors of x in the product, so the exponent is 3 + 4.|Negative exponent~An exponent indicating a reciprocal power.;Power of a power~An expression whose exponents multiply when simplified.''')
extra('algebra2','Polynomials & complex numbers','''Quadratics with nonreal solutions|Completing the square and the quadratic formula work beyond real solutions when complex numbers are used. For real coefficients, nonreal roots occur as conjugates. Keep the full denominator in the quadratic formula and simplify radicals before stating a + bi form.|x² + 4 = 0 gives x² = −4 and x = ±2i.|Solve x² + 9 = 0 in the complex numbers.|x = ±3i|x = ±3~x = 9i only~No complex solutions|Since i² = −1, both 3i and −3i square to −9.|Nonreal root~A complex solution with nonzero imaginary part.;Conjugate-root pair~Paired roots a + bi and a − bi for a real-coefficient polynomial.''')
extra('geometry','Right triangles & trigonometry','''Area and nonright triangle relationships|A triangle's area is one half of two side lengths times the sine of their included angle. More general triangle relationships extend right-triangle reasoning: the law of cosines relates three sides and an included angle, while the law of sines relates corresponding side-angle ratios.|Sides 10 and 8 with included angle 30° give area (1/2)(10)(8)sin30° = 20.|Two sides 6 and 10 include a 90° angle. Their triangle area is…|30|60~16~8|Use A = ab sin(C)/2. Here sin90° = 1, giving 6 × 10/2.|Included angle~The angle between two specified sides.;Law of cosines~c² = a² + b² − 2ab cos C for the side opposite C.''')
extra('biology','Ecosystem dynamics','''Carbon-system models and group behavior|Carbon moves among atmosphere, organisms, oceans, soil, and rocks. A model must show transfers rather than create carbon when biomass grows. Organism groups can improve survival through cooperation, defense, or heat conservation, but group effects depend on environmental context and evidence.|Photosynthesis transfers atmospheric carbon into biomass; huddling can reduce heat loss in cold conditions.|Which group behavior can reduce heat loss in cold environments?|Huddling|Converting sunlight directly into fur~Stopping all respiration~Creating carbon atoms|Close grouping can reduce exposed surface and alter heat exchange; the benefit depends on conditions.|Carbon reservoir~A place storing carbon within the Earth system.;Group behavior~Coordinated or socially influenced activity affecting organisms' outcomes.''')
extra('biology','Evolution & diversity','''Life and Earth change together|Organisms alter environments, which in turn change selection pressures. Photosynthetic life contributed to atmospheric oxygen changes, affecting metabolism and other Earth processes. This is a feedback across biological and physical systems, not an organism intentionally redesigning its environment.|Increasing oxygen availability supported some metabolic pathways while challenging organisms adapted to low-oxygen conditions.|Which illustrates interaction between life and Earth's physical systems?|Photosynthesis changing atmospheric composition|Organisms creating every element~Geology never affecting life~Evolution stopping all environmental change|Biological activity can change atmospheric chemistry, which influences later life and Earth processes.|Coevolution of Earth and life~Reciprocal changes in biological and physical Earth systems.;Selection pressure~An environmental influence on relative reproductive success.''')
extra('earth','Solar system & orbital patterns','''Tides and gravitational differences|Tides arise from differences in gravitational influence across Earth, especially from the Moon and also the Sun. Earth's rotation carries locations through changing tidal patterns. Alignment of Sun, Earth, and Moon affects tidal range; local coastline and seafloor shape actual timing and height.|New and full Moon alignments generally produce greater tidal ranges than quarter Moon configurations.|Spring tides are associated with…|Sun-Earth-Moon alignment|The spring season only~The absence of lunar gravity~Identical tides at every coast|Aligned solar and lunar tidal effects generally increase the range between high and low water.|Tidal range~The difference between high and low water levels.;Spring tide~A relatively large tidal range associated with solar-lunar alignment.''')
extra('earth','Weather & atmospheric circulation','''Oceans redistribute energy|Surface currents respond to winds, rotation, and continents. Density differences related to temperature and salinity contribute to deeper circulation. Ocean circulation redistributes energy and influences regional climate. A warming or freshening region can change density and alter patterns without stopping every current.|Warm water transported poleward can moderate nearby climates.|Ocean circulation affects climate partly by…|Redistributing thermal energy|Creating extra solar radiation~Removing all atmospheric moisture~Making every latitude equally warm|Transport moves energy between regions, while local conditions and feedbacks still differ.|Ocean circulation~Large-scale movement of ocean water.;Thermohaline circulation~Density-related circulation influenced by temperature and salinity.''')
extra('global','Tradition, modernization & political change','''Turkey and Iran in comparative context|Modernization can provoke debates over religion, state authority, gender, and foreign influence. Atatürk's reforms promoted secular national institutions in Turkey. Iran's 1979 revolution challenged the shah and produced an Islamic republic. Compare causes and outcomes without assuming religion and modernization always have one relationship.|Different political movements can use claims of national independence while proposing very different institutions.|Which approach best compares Turkey's reforms and Iran's revolution?|Analyze each movement's context and institutional goals|Assume identical outcomes~Treat all religious people as one actor~Ignore foreign influence|Comparison requires specific evidence about distinct settings, aims, and political changes.|Secularism~An approach limiting religious authority in state institutions.;Modernization~Changes in institutions, economy, or technology associated with modern social organization.
China under Mao and Deng|Mao-era campaigns sought rapid transformation through centralized mobilization; the Great Leap Forward and Cultural Revolution caused profound disruption and suffering. Deng-era reforms expanded market mechanisms and international economic links while one-party control continued. Economic change did not automatically mean political liberalization.|An economy can adopt market incentives without creating competitive multiparty elections.|A major distinction in Deng-era China was…|Market-oriented economic reforms under continued party rule|An immediate end to party authority~A return to all Mao-era economic policies unchanged~Complete isolation from international trade|Economic reform and political pluralism are separate dimensions of change.|Cultural Revolution~A Mao-era political campaign involving major social and institutional upheaval.;Market reform~Policy expanding market mechanisms in economic activity.''',new_after=8)
extra('us','Revolution & constitutional foundations','''Early republic and constitutional precedents|Hamilton and Jefferson disagreed over finance, economic development, and constitutional interpretation. Washington established executive precedents; Marshall Court decisions developed federal and judicial authority. Political participation expanded unevenly, while Indigenous removal and enslavement exposed deep contradictions in national ideals.|Marbury v. Madison established a major precedent for federal judicial review.|Judicial review concerns the courts' power to…|Evaluate constitutionality of government action|Appoint every legislator~Write every treaty~Collect taxes directly from all states|Judicial review evaluates actions against constitutional requirements rather than replacing every other branch's function.|Precedent~An earlier decision or practice guiding later action.;Strict construction~A narrow approach to interpreting constitutional grants of power.''')
extra('us','Reconstruction & its limits','''Western settlement and Indigenous dispossession|Railroads, mining, agriculture, and federal policy transformed the West after the Civil War. Settlement involved coercive removal, military conflict, treaty violations, and assimilation policies toward Indigenous nations. Interpret national expansion alongside loss of land, sovereignty, and cultural suppression.|The Dawes Act divided tribal lands into individual allotments and undermined collective landholding.|Which consequence is associated with allotment policies toward Native Americans?|Loss of tribal land and pressure toward assimilation|Universal protection of tribal sovereignty~An end to all federal involvement~The absence of land transfers|Allotment policies disrupted collective landholding and enabled substantial transfers away from Indigenous control.|Assimilation~Pressure or change toward adopting a dominant group's cultural practices.;Allotment~Division of collectively held land into individual parcels.''')
extra('ela','Research, discussion & other writing','''Research questions and source credibility|An inquiry begins with a focused question that can be investigated. Evaluate sources by evidence, methods, expertise, purpose, and corroboration. Record citations while researching and distinguish paraphrase from quotation. A prestigious appearance is not a substitute for a traceable evidentiary basis.|A focused question asks how one policy affected a specified population over a defined period.|Which source feature most strengthens credibility for a factual claim?|Transparent methods and corroborating evidence|A large decorative logo~Agreement with the reader alone~An unsupported anonymous assertion|Credibility depends on evidence and accountability, not presentation or preference.|Research question~A focused question guiding an investigation.;Paraphrase~Restating a source's meaning accurately in one's own words.
Discussion and presentation|Effective discussion builds on others' ideas, asks clarifying questions, and supports disagreements with evidence. A presentation adapts organization, language, and media to purpose and audience. Evaluate listening and reasoning as well as delivery; persuasive confidence alone does not make a claim accurate.|A speaker can acknowledge an objection, restate it fairly, and answer it with relevant evidence.|Which response best advances an evidence-based discussion?|Ask a clarifying question and address the supporting evidence|Interrupt with a personal insult~Repeat a claim louder~Ignore a relevant counterexample|Productive discussion tests ideas through listening, clarification, and supported reasoning.|Audience~The people for whom a communication is intended.;Clarifying question~A question seeking a more precise understanding.
Narrative and explanatory writing|Narratives develop experiences through purposeful detail, perspective, pacing, and reflection. Explanatory writing organizes information to develop understanding. Both require coherence and revision, but they serve different purposes. Borrowed facts and language still need accurate source treatment.|A process explanation organizes causes or stages; a narrative can organize events around a developing conflict.|What most distinguishes explanatory writing's central purpose?|Developing the reader's understanding of a topic|Inventing a conflict in every paragraph~Avoiding all evidence~Persuading through unsupported claims|Explanatory writing aims to clarify information and relationships rather than necessarily argue a position or tell a story.|Explanatory writing~Writing organized to develop understanding of information or ideas.;Narrative~An account developing events or experiences through selected details.''',new_after=7)
# Reassign stable IDs after deliberate sequence insertions.
for c in COURSES:
 for n,u in enumerate(c['units'],1):
  u['number']=n;u['id']=f"{c['id']}-u{n}"
  for j,l in enumerate(u['lessons'],1):l['id']=f"{u['id']}-l{j}"
if __name__=='__main__':
 Path('curriculum/ny-authored.json').write_text(json.dumps(COURSES,ensure_ascii=False,indent=2)+'\n')
 print(len(COURSES),'courses,',sum(len(c['units'])for c in COURSES),'units,',sum(len(u['lessons'])for c in COURSES for u in c['units']),'lessons')
